#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Alert when an agent lane on this machine has stopped: a dead Envoy role holder, or an agent
tmux pane left sitting at a shell.

Part one, the Envoy role check.

Why this exists: on 2026-10-27T05:52Z the Verified Linux owner session
(01a1798a-9911-7231-a1bb-cf29d7985273, role lead-verified-linux) stopped, wrote no error and
no exit record, and nothing noticed. Its Envoy role claim stayed registered, so messages to
notifications.role.lead-verified-linux looked deliverable; about 40 in-session subagent lanes
were lost, and a peer first learnt of it 11 minutes later from a "no live session" send error.

What one run does, mechanically, with no model call:

  1. GET /v1/sessions  -- every session Envoy lists as live, with its roles, host, dir and the
     timestamp of its last heartbeat.
  2. For each remembered role, first check the holder's current host in the session list or
     the point registry. Skip a holder registered on another host, regardless of heartbeat age.
     For a local holder, establish what can be established from four
     sources: an `omp` process that names the session on its command line, in the file name of
     an open transcript, or in that transcript's own header; any process at all holding the
     session's transcript open; how long ago that transcript was last written, in the session
     root or in the holder's own working directory; and Envoy's own registry entry and
     heartbeat (GET /v1/sessions, confirmed by GET /v1/registry/<id>, both of which only read).
     When all four are silent, a fifth reads the holder's WORKING DIRECTORY: the newest regular
     file under it, named, with an absolute mtime. That one is never a sighting and never says
     the lane works -- see DEFAULT_DIR_GRACE -- but it is the only thing that can end an alert
     about a holder none of the four can ever see, which on 2026-10-30 was four alerts in 33
     minutes on a role claimed by a process that is not an omp session.
     Before alerting on a holder absent from the registry, check the other developer hosts
     with `ssh <host> omp-session-live <id>`, under a short timeout. Each alert retains the
     checked hosts and the approximate time of the next reading.
  3. The watch calls no role route at all. GET /v1/roles/<role> is how Envoy releases a claim
     whose holder has left the session bucket (role_holder.go resolveLiveRoleHolder, still so on
     origin/main at d157fc868, checked 2026-10-29 09:50Z), and that holder is exactly the one
     this watch would ever ask about: a read that is a write. On 2026-10-29 the alert text named
     it, a reader ran it, and a live lane lost its claim two minutes after a false alert. Even
     once it is pure it buys little -- the claim says only that it is registered to the session
     the watch already cannot find -- so what the watch gives up with it is in `alert_text`.
  4. A holder is only ever UNSEEN, never dead. From a watcher's position "I cannot find it" is
     the strongest thing establishable: a loaded box stops heartbeating inside Envoy's 300 s TTL
     (a root session and 18 subagents measured silent for 17 minutes at load 233), and omp writes
     no exit record, so nothing here can prove a session ended. Every alert says which of the two
     it established, and `guard_verdict` refuses to send one that claims the holder is dead.
     A role alerts only when EVERY source is silent for the grace window (default 60 s); one
     silent source -- most often Envoy's registry, which drops live sessions under load -- is
     logged and never alerts, never opens an incident and never asks anyone to restart anything.

Alerts go over Envoy to agent sessions only, never to a person: the chief of staff, the INFRA
lead, the dead role's stream lead (the live `lead-*`/`stream-lead-*` holder in the same working
directory), and the lane's peers (the live holders of the team topics the dead session shared).
One alert per incident, one more when it ends -- seen again, quieted on the directory rule, or
closed because its class is suppressed -- and one escalation to the chief of staff when an
incident is still open 10 minutes after its alert. A suppression (SUPPRESSIONS) is named, dated
and counted in every alert of every other class while it holds, and says EXPIRED in the same
places once its date passes.

Part two, the tmux pane check (`check_panes`). After the 2026-10-27T13:56Z reboot, five of six
Verified Linux panes came back as a bash prompt: tmux-resurrect restored the layout but their omp
never started. No process died, so nothing above sees it, and only one of the six held a role. So
every pane of the default tmux server is read directly: an AGENT pane is one whose title omp set
(it starts "π "), or one this watch has seen omp running in, or one with an omp among the
descendants of its pane process. It is AT A SHELL when the pane process is a shell and is its own
terminal foreground process group -- nothing is running in it at all. After a 180 s grace that is
one alert naming the pane, the session it last ran, its handover files and the exact
`tmux send-keys` that restarts it, to the INFRA lead and the lane's stream lead; one clear when
omp runs there again or the pane goes away. The session is resolved from what this watch saw
running in the pane, else the transcript in the pane's directory whose own title the pane title
carries, else the `omp --resume` command tmux-resurrect saved for that pane's place.

A pane counts only when its last omp died without meaning to (`pane_release`): not when its
session's transcript ends in a normal session_exit, not when the omp was a one-shot (`omp -p`),
not while an `agentbox move` owns the session or once it has moved it away from this machine.
What it still catches is an omp that died without a normal exit. And it alerts once per pane per
session until omp runs in that pane again: a pane whose session was seen running elsewhere is no
longer that lane's pane. Pane %40 cleared and alerted twice each between 06:48Z and 07:09Z on
2026-11-03 because annotation runs that only named its session read as the session running
elsewhere; such a run no longer counts as the session (`runs_no_session`).

The watcher never executes that command. A pane a person paused and a pane whose omp died look
the same to a script, so the person or lead who reads the alert looks at the pane and decides.

Scope: role holders, and panes. A session that holds no role and has no pane is not watched.

Four rules about what a run costs and what it says, all of them from measurements on
sami-rocqperf in the 48 hours to 2026-11-03 (dispatch://INFRA-672):

  A RUN HAS A BUDGET (--budget, 60 s inside the unit's 90 s TimeoutStartSec), counted from the
  moment the process started. Past it the run stops between two units of work, saves what it has
  decided, logs one line saying where it stopped and exits 0, so systemd records no failure and
  the next period starts with the roles this one did not reach. 35 of 545 runs in six hours were
  killed at the timeout, each one a period in which nothing after the slow step ran at all. The
  steps that took them there are gone: one pass over /proc instead of two, the transcript
  headers kept between runs in headers.json, and no walk, descriptor scan or Postgres read for
  an incident that is finished.

  EVERY DISK AND /proc READ HAS A HARD LIMIT OF ITS OWN. It runs in a forked child (see
  `bounded_step`), and one that has not answered at its limit is cut: the pane or role it was
  for is not judged this period -- no alert, no clear -- with one line, and the run goes on.
  A budget the run checks between two units of work did not stop the three runs killed at 90 s
  on 2026-11-03 between 07:14Z and 07:22Z, which logged nothing at all.

  THE JOURNAL SAYS WHAT CHANGED. One summary line a run, one line when a pane or a role changes
  state, and the alerts and clears, which are events. The state behind each line is in the state
  file under `logged`. It was 150,188 lines in 40 hours, the same sentences every 30 seconds.

  A MESSAGE IS AT MOST 600 CHARACTERS and ends with `Full report: <path>`. The message is the
  decision; the report file beside the state file holds the whole text, and reports older than
  seven days are deleted in the same run. A pane alert was 2,404 characters.
"""

