            " suppression covers the days it takes to watch that rule against real runs"
        ),
    },
)

# THE MARKED PLACE FOR THE RELEASE CALL. Agent infra is adding an explicit release that records
# who released the claim, and the same change makes GET /v1/roles/<role> pure. Neither exists
# yet. Checked 2026-10-29 09:50Z, both ways:
#   - POST /v1/roles/release on the live listener answers 405.
#   - sjawhar/legion origin/main at d157fc868 has no roles/release handler anywhere, and
#     packages/envoy/cmd/listener/role_holder.go resolveLiveRoleHolder still calls
#     releaseExpiredRoleClaim for a holder that has left the session bucket -- which is the only
#     holder anyone reads this claim for. The lookup is still a write.
# When the route lands, this constant becomes the two sentences below and nothing else in
# `alert_text` moves. `{role}` and `{holder}` are filled in there, so a curl body's braces are
# doubled:
#   RELEASE_CALL = (
#       "RELEASE THE CLAIM, only once step 2 or 3 has established that no live lane holds it:"
#       " `curl -sS -X POST \"${{ENVOY_URL:-http://127.0.0.1:9020}}/v1/roles/release\""
#       " -H 'Content-Type: application/json'"
#       " -d '{{\"role\": \"{role}\", \"holder\": \"{holder}\","
#       " \"released_by\": \"<your session id or name>\"}}'`."
#       " Reading the claim changes nothing then:"
#       " `curl -sS \"${{ENVOY_URL:-http://127.0.0.1:9020}}/v1/roles/{role}\"`."
#   )
RELEASE_CALL = (
    "RELEASE THE CLAIM: there is no call for this yet, so this watch names none. Do NOT reach"
    " for the two that look like one. `curl ${{ENVOY_URL}}/v1/roles/{role}` releases a lapsed"
    " holder's claim as a side effect of answering (resolveLiveRoleHolder), and"
    " `curl -X DELETE ${{ENVOY_URL}}/v1/sessions/{holder}` unregisters a session that may be"
    " alive; together they are what cost role bet64-eta-arity its claim at 2026-10-29 09:22:31Z,"
    " two minutes after a false alert on a live lane. Let the claim lapse on its own, or wait"
    " for agent infra's explicit release route,"
    " which this text will name as soon as it is deployed."
)


def log(message: str) -> None:
    stamp = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())
    print(f"{stamp} {message}", flush=True)


# The journal keys (`role:<role>`, `pane:<tmux pane key>`) whose state this run decided.
_LOG_KEYS: set[str] = set()


def log_change(state, key: str, signature: str, message: str = "") -> None:
    """Log `message` only when `key`'s state differs from the one the last run recorded.

    The state file keeps one signature per pane and per role under `logged`. A signature names
    the state and nothing that moves on its own -- never an age, never a heartbeat -- so a role
    whose holder runs on writes one `ok` line when it becomes ok and none after, where it wrote
    one every 30 s: 10,723 role lines and 8,318 pane lines in six hours on 2026-11-02/03, of
    23,597. An empty `message` records the state without a line, for the states whose line is
    an event already logged (an ALERT, a CLEARED) or a healthy pane, which never had one.
    """
    logged = state.data.setdefault("logged", {})
    _LOG_KEYS.add(key)
    if logged.get(key) == signature:
        return
    logged[key] = signature
    if message:
        log(message)


def forget_logged(state, prefix: str) -> None:
    """Drop the recorded journal states under `prefix` that this run did not decide: the pane or
    role is gone, and if it comes back its first state is news again."""
    logged = state.data.get("logged") or {}
    for key in [key for key in logged if key.startswith(prefix) and key not in _LOG_KEYS]:
        logged.pop(key)


# The run's deadline, wall clock: `_RUN["deadline"]`, set by run_once from --budget.
_RUN: dict = {"deadline": float("inf")}


class OutOfTime(Exception):
    """The run reached its budget. Raised only between two units of work -- a process, a pane,
    a role, a source -- never inside one, so what a run stops on is left as the last run did."""


def check_time(where: str) -> None:
    if time.time() > _RUN["deadline"]:
        raise OutOfTime(where)


def process_started() -> float:
    """When this process started, from /proc. The budget counts Python's own start-up because
    systemd's TimeoutStartSec counts it too, and under load it is not small."""
    try:
        ticks = int(Path("/proc/self/stat").read_text().rpartition(")")[2].split()[19])
        with open("/proc/stat") as handle:
            boot = next(int(line.split()[1]) for line in handle if line.startswith("btime "))
        return min(time.time(), boot + ticks / os.sysconf("SC_CLK_TCK"))
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError, StopIteration):
        return time.time()


# ---------------------------------------------- the disk and /proc steps, bounded from outside
#
# A clock a step checks itself is not enough. The three runs killed at the unit's 90 s on
# 2026-11-03 (started 07:14:50Z, 07:19:21Z, 07:20:56Z, at io some 87% with 305 tasks blocked)
# logged nothing at all, while the runs between them finished in 2-12 s. A read the box has
# stalled does not come back to look at a clock: the kernel stack of this unit's run sampled at
# 10:54:32Z the same day was waiting on the root disk inside a stat, for the block of a symlink
# (ext4_get_link, __wait_on_buffer); the transcript headers, /proc/<pid> files and directory
# listings read here can wait as long. So every step that reads the disk or /proc
# runs in a forked child (`bounded_step`), and the run stops waiting for it at a hard
# limit: STEP_SECONDS, or the budget left when that is less. A child that has not answered is
# sent SIGKILL and not waited for; one stuck in a read the kernel will not interrupt goes with
# the unit's cgroup once the run exits. The step is then CUT: the pane or role it was for is
# skipped with one journal line and nothing decided for it -- no alert, no clear -- and the
# next period reads it again. A cut process scan stops the run as the budget does, since a half
# table would make live holders look unseen. The run itself goes on and exits 0.
STEP_SECONDS = 15.0
# Under this, a step is not started at all: it is cut before it begins.
STEP_MINIMUM = 0.5
# What a child fills in for the run, sent back with its answer so the run goes on with it: the
# memo tables it updates in place, and the module values it builds once per run.
_MEMO_TABLES = (
    "_HEADER_IDS", "_HEADER_USED", "_KNOWN_TRANSCRIPTS", "_TRANSCRIPT_WALK", "_SWEPT",
    "_BOX_LAUNCHERS", "_OMP_PIDS", "_SCAN",
)
_MEMO_VALUES = ("_TRANSCRIPT_INDEX", "_SWEEP_INDEX", "_PROC_TABLE", "_OPEN_JSONL")
# `bounded`: set by run_once, so a direct call (a test, a hand session) runs a step in this
# process. `child`: set in a step's own child, where a nested bounded step runs in place.
# `lock_fd`: the state file's locked descriptor, which a child closes, because a child the
# kernel holds past this run would otherwise keep the lock and the next run would skip its
# period. `cut`: the steps this run cut, for its summary line.
_STEPS: dict = {"bounded": False, "child": False, "lock_fd": None, "cut": []}


class StepCut(OutOfTime):
    """A bounded step did not answer inside its limit. The unit of work it was for is skipped
    with nothing decided; anything that catches OutOfTime stops on it as on the budget."""


def bounded_step(name: str, whole_budget: bool = False):
    """Run the decorated step in a forked child under a hard limit: STEP_SECONDS, or the run's
    budget left when that is less (`whole_budget`: the budget left only)."""

    def wrap(function):
        @functools.wraps(function)
        def call(*args, **kwargs):
            if not _STEPS["bounded"] or _STEPS["child"]:
                return function(*args, **kwargs)
            return run_bounded(name, whole_budget, function, args, kwargs)

        return call

    return wrap


def run_bounded(name: str, whole_budget: bool, function, args: tuple, kwargs: dict):
    left = _RUN["deadline"] - time.time()
    limit = left if whole_budget else min(STEP_SECONDS, left)
    if limit <= STEP_MINIMUM:
        _STEPS["cut"].append(name)
        raise StepCut(f"{name}, not started: the run has {max(left, 0):.0f} s of its budget left")
    sys.stdout.flush()
    read_fd, write_fd = os.pipe()
    pid = os.fork()
    if pid == 0:
        os.close(read_fd)
        answer_from_child(write_fd, function, args, kwargs)
    os.close(write_fd)
    chunks: list[bytes] = []
    answered = False
    until = time.monotonic() + limit
    with os.fdopen(read_fd, "rb", buffering=0) as source:
        while True:
            remaining = until - time.monotonic()
            if remaining <= 0 or not select.select([source], [], [], remaining)[0]:
                break
            chunk = source.read(1 << 16)
            if not chunk:
                answered = True
                break
            chunks.append(chunk)
    if not answered:
        try:
            os.kill(pid, signal.SIGKILL)
        except OSError:
            pass
        _STEPS["cut"].append(name)
        raise StepCut(
            f"{name}, cut after {limit:.0f} s still reading (a disk or /proc read the box has stalled)"
        )
    os.waitpid(pid, 0)  # it closed its end, so it is already leaving
    try:
        kind, value, memo, lines = pickle.loads(b"".join(chunks))
    except (pickle.UnpicklingError, EOFError, ValueError, TypeError) as error:
        raise RuntimeError(f"{name}: no answer it could read from its child ({error})") from None
    module = globals()
    for table, contents in memo.items():
        if table in _MEMO_TABLES:
            module[table].clear()
            module[table].update(contents)
        else:
            module[table] = contents
    for line in lines:
        log(line)
    if kind == "raise":
        raise value
    return value


def answer_from_child(write_fd: int, function, args: tuple, kwargs: dict) -> None:
    """In the child: run the step, send back (kind, value, memo tables it changed, the lines it
    logged), and leave without running any exit handler. Never returns."""
    module = globals()
    _STEPS["child"] = True
    if _STEPS["lock_fd"] is not None:
        try:
            os.close(_STEPS["lock_fd"])
        except OSError:
            pass
    lines: list[str] = []
    module["log"] = lines.append
    tables = {table: dict(module[table]) for table in _MEMO_TABLES}
    values = {name: module[name] for name in _MEMO_VALUES}
    try:
        outcome = ("return", function(*args, **kwargs))
    except BaseException as error:  # noqa: BLE001 -- raised again in the run, never here
        outcome = ("raise", error)
    memo = {table: module[table] for table in _MEMO_TABLES if module[table] != tables[table]}
    memo.update({name: module[name] for name in _MEMO_VALUES if module[name] is not values[name]})
    try:
        payload = pickle.dumps((*outcome, memo, lines))
    except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001
        payload = pickle.dumps(("raise", RuntimeError(f"its answer could not be sent back: {error}"), {}, lines))
    try:
        with os.fdopen(write_fd, "wb") as answer:
            answer.write(payload)
    finally:
        os._exit(0)


def iso(epoch: float | None) -> str:
    if not epoch:
        return "unknown"
    return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(epoch))


def human(seconds: float) -> str:
    seconds = int(seconds)
    if seconds < 90:
        return f"{seconds} s"
    minutes, rest = divmod(seconds, 60)
    if minutes < 90:
        return f"{minutes} min {rest} s"
    hours, minutes = divmod(minutes, 60)
    return f"{hours} h {minutes} min"


def epoch_of(stamp: str) -> float:
    """`2026-11-06T00:00:00Z` as a UTC epoch. Absolute, never relative: a date in the file is a
    date a reader can check against `date -u`, and `find -newermt '-3 minutes'` returning
    nothing for a file written one second ago (measured 2026-10-30 22:31Z) is what relative
    times are worth."""
    return calendar.timegm(time.strptime(stamp, "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ"))


def suppression_for(role: str, now: float) -> dict | None:
    """The suppression that silences `role` right now, or None once it has expired."""
    for rule in SUPPRESSIONS:
        if rule["pattern"].match(role or "") and now < epoch_of(rule["until"]):
            return rule
    return None


def suppression_lines(now: float, counts: dict | None = None, short: bool = False) -> list[str]:
    """What every alert of every other class, and every run's log, says about the suppressions.

    A suppression nobody can see is the same silent training with a different author, so this
    goes out with the alerts a reader already reads, naming the pattern, the date, the reason
    and how many roles it has actually swallowed. Past the date it says EXPIRED in the same
    place, every run, until the entry is deleted.

    `short` is the journal's form: the same facts without the reason, because the long one is
    700 characters and the timer writes it every 30 s. The reason belongs in the alerts, which
    a person reads once.
    """
    lines: list[str] = []
    for rule in SUPPRESSIONS:
        seen = (counts or {}).get(rule["shown"]) or {}
        tally = (
            f" It has suppressed {seen.get('count', 0)} role(s) so far, last"
            f" {seen.get('role') or '-'} at {iso(seen.get('at'))}."
            if seen else " It has suppressed no role yet."
        )
        if short:
            state = "ACTIVE" if now < epoch_of(rule["until"]) else "EXPIRED, DELETE THE ENTRY"
            lines.append(
                f"SUPPRESSION {state}: {rule['shown']} until {rule['until']}.{tally}"
            )
            continue
        if now < epoch_of(rule["until"]):
            lines.append(
                f"ACTIVE SUPPRESSION ({rule['name']}): role-holder alerts for roles matching"
                f" {rule['shown']} are not sent until {rule['until']}. Why: {rule['why']}."
                f"{tally} Every other role alerts normally."
            )
        else:
            lines.append(
                f"EXPIRED SUPPRESSION ({rule['name']}): roles matching {rule['shown']} were"
                f" suppressed until {rule['until']}, which has passed, so the class alerts again"
                f" under the ordinary rules from this run on.{tally} This line repeats in every"
                " alert and every run's log until the entry is deleted from SUPPRESSIONS in"
                " ~/.dotfiles/scripts/envoy-role-holder-watch."
            )
    return lines


def suppression_note(now: float) -> list[str]:
    """The suppressions in one short sentence each, for a message whose report has the long form."""
    notes = []
    for rule in SUPPRESSIONS:
        if now < epoch_of(rule["until"]):
            notes.append(f"Suppressed until {rule['until']}: roles matching {rule['shown']} (see the report).")
        else:
            notes.append(f"EXPIRED suppression {rule['shown']}: delete the entry (see the report).")
    return notes


# ------------------------------------------------------------------- messages and reports


def write_report(options: argparse.Namespace, kind: str, text: str, now: float) -> Path:
    """Write the whole text of one message to <state dir>/reports/<UTC stamp>-<kind>.txt.

    The message itself carries the decision and names this file last; the file carries every
    line the message used to: sources, steps, recipients, the suppression's reason. Reports
    older than REPORT_KEEP are pruned in the same call. A dry run that writes no state writes no
    report either, and the message names the path it would have had.
    """
    directory = Path(options.state).parent / "reports"
    stamp = time.strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ", time.gmtime(now))
    name = re.sub(r"[^A-Za-z0-9._-]+", "_", kind).strip("_")[:80] or "report"
    path = directory / f"{stamp}-{name}.txt"
    if options.dry_run and not getattr(options, "write_state", False):
        return path
    try:
        directory.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        number = 1
        while path.exists():
            number += 1
            path = directory / f"{stamp}-{name}-{number}.txt"
        path.write_text(text + "\n")
    except OSError as error:
        log(f"report {path} not written: {error}")
    try:
        with os.scandir(directory) as entries:
            for entry in entries:
                try:
                    if entry.name.endswith(".txt") and entry.stat().st_mtime < now - REPORT_KEEP:
                        os.unlink(entry.path)
                except OSError:
                    continue
    except OSError:
        pass
    return path


def short_message(report: Path, *parts: str, required: tuple[str, ...] = ()) -> str:
    """At most MESSAGE_LIMIT characters: `parts` in order while they fit, then the report path.

    The first part is the decision -- what, where, how long, who acts -- and is always there,
    cut if it must be; every later part is kept whole or left out, so a long working directory
    drops a closing sentence rather than leave half of one.
    """
    tail = "".join("\n" + line for line in required) + f"\nFull report: {report}"
    room = MESSAGE_LIMIT - len(tail)
    head = ""
    for index, part in enumerate(" ".join(part.split()) for part in parts if part):
        joined = f"{head} {part}" if head else part
        if len(joined) <= room:
            head = joined
        elif index == 0:
            head = part[: max(0, room - 3)].rstrip() + "..."
    return guard_verdict(head + tail)


def next_reading(now: float) -> str:
    return f"Next reading: {iso(max(now, time.time()) + 30)} (about 30 s). Please wait one cycle before escalating."


def compose(
    options: argparse.Namespace, kind: str, full: str, now: float, *parts: str,
    required: tuple[str, ...] = (),
) -> tuple[str, Path]:
    """Keep the next reading and checked hosts even when the decision must be shortened."""
    required = (*required, next_reading(now))
    full += "\n" + "\n".join(required)
    report = write_report(options, kind, guard_verdict(full), now)
    return short_message(report, *parts, required=required), report


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- Envoy transport


def named_recipient(fallback: str, sessions: list[dict]) -> str:
    """Resolve a duty without the side effects of GET /v1/roles/<role>."""
    role = NAMED_ROLES.get(fallback)
    return next(
        (s["session_id"] for s in sessions if role in (s.get("roles") or []) and s.get("session_id")),
        fallback,
    ) if role else fallback


class Envoy:
    """The listener this machine's sessions use, resolved the way scripts/envoy resolves it."""

    def __init__(self, timeout: float = 8.0) -> None:
        path = os.environ.get("ENVOY_CONFIG_FILE") or str(
            Path(os.environ.get("XDG_CONFIG_HOME", str(Path.home() / ".config")))
            / "legion/envoy.json"
        )
        config: dict = {}
        try:
            config = json.loads(Path(path).read_text()).get("envoy") or {}
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            config = {}
        if config.get("enabled") is False:
            raise SystemExit(f"{path} sets envoy.enabled to false: Envoy is off")
        self.url = (os.environ.get("ENVOY_URL") or config.get("url") or DEFAULT_ENVOY_URL).rstrip("/")
        self.timeout = timeout
        token = os.environ.get("ENVOY_TOKEN") or config.get("token") or ""
        if token.startswith("!"):
            # An envoy.json token may be a command; run it the way envoy-client does, and never
            # print either of its streams.
            try:
                done = subprocess.run(
                    ["/bin/sh", "-c", token[1:]],
                    capture_output=True,
                    text=True,
                    timeout=10,
                    stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                )
                token = done.stdout.strip() if done.returncode == 0 else ""
            except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
                token = ""
        self.token = token

    def request(self, method: str, path: str, body: dict | None = None) -> tuple[int, dict | list]:
        data = json.dumps(body).encode() if body is not None else None
        request = urllib.request.Request(self.url + path, data=data, method=method)
        if data is not None:
            request.add_header("Content-Type", "application/json")
        if self.token:
            request.add_header("Authorization", f"Bearer {self.token}")
        try:
            with urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=self.timeout) as answer:
                raw = answer.read().decode() or "{}"
                return answer.status, json.loads(raw)
        except urllib.error.HTTPError as error:
            raw = error.read().decode() or "{}"
            try:
                return error.code, json.loads(raw)
            except ValueError:
                return error.code, {"error": raw.strip()}
        except (urllib.error.URLError, socket.timeout, ValueError, OSError) as error:
            return 0, {"error": str(error)}

    def sessions(self) -> list[dict]:
        status, body = self.request("GET", "/v1/sessions")
        if status != 200 or not isinstance(body, list):
            raise RuntimeError(f"GET /v1/sessions: {status} {body}")
        self.live_sessions = body
        return body

    # There is deliberately no role() here, for two reasons, and the first still holds today.
    # GET /v1/roles/<role> releases a claim whose holder has left the session bucket
    # (cmd/listener/role_holder.go resolveLiveRoleHolder -> releaseExpiredRoleClaim; still so on
    # sjawhar/legion origin/main at d157fc868, and the live listener answers 405 to the
    # POST /v1/roles/release that will replace it -- both checked 2026-10-29 09:50Z). That
    # holder is the only one this watch would ask about, so the read is a write. And even once
    # it is pure, its answer adds nothing this watch judges on: the claim's holder is the
    # session it has already failed to find.

    def registry(self, session_id: str) -> tuple[int, dict]:
        """Envoy's own registry entry for one session: a point read that writes nothing.

        `sessionEntryHandler` reads the session bucket and answers it (cmd/listener/api.go on
        sjawhar/legion origin/main at d157fc868, checked 2026-10-29 09:50Z). It shares that read
        with the role lookup but not the release the role lookup makes around it, so unlike the
        role lookup it is safe against a holder that has lapsed. 404 is Envoy saying it cannot
        see the session either; 200 says the /v1/sessions list this run read was stale.
        """
        status, body = self.request("GET", f"/v1/registry/{session_id}")
        return status, body if isinstance(body, dict) else {"error": str(body)}

    def send(self, session_id: str, message: str) -> bool:
        session_id = named_recipient(session_id, getattr(self, "live_sessions", []))
        status, body = self.request(
            "POST",
            "/v1/messages/send",
            {"target_session": session_id, "message": message, "source": "envoy"},
        )
        confirmed = (status == 200 and isinstance(body, dict) and bool(body.get("event_id"))
                     and body.get("topic") == f"notifications.agent.{session_id}")
        if not confirmed:
            log(f"  send to {session_id} unconfirmed: {status} {body}")
        return confirmed

    def publish(self, topic: str, message: str) -> bool:
        status, body = self.request(
            "POST", "/v1/messages/publish", {"topic": topic, "message": message, "source": "envoy"}
        )
        confirmed = (status == 200 and isinstance(body, dict) and bool(body.get("event_id"))
                     and body.get("topic") == topic)
        if not confirmed:
            log(f"  publish to {topic} unconfirmed: {status} {body}")
        return confirmed


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- host ownership


def short_host(host: str) -> str:
    return host.split(".")[0]


def session_host(session: dict) -> str:
    # machine_id is usually the listener (envoy-hub), not the session's physical host.
    return short_host(session.get("host") or session.get("machine_id") or "")


def local_hostnames(host: str, sessions: list[dict]) -> set[str]:
    """The host and its boxes, including boxes started before host registration was fixed."""
    names = {short_host(host)}
    if any(session_host(session).startswith("agentbox-") for session in sessions):
        try:
            done = subprocess.run(
                ["docker", "ps", "--format", "{{.Names}}"], capture_output=True,
                text=True, timeout=REMOTE_SECONDS, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            )
            if done.returncode == 0:
                names.update(short_host(name) for name in done.stdout.splitlines() if name)
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
            pass  # An unknown box is not judged here. Its next registration can identify it.
    return names


def remote_presence(holder: str, host: str, hosts: list[str]) -> tuple[str, dict[str, str]]:
    """A live host, if found, and each SSH result. An SSH failure is not an absence."""
    checked: dict[str, str] = {}
    for target in dict.fromkeys(hosts):
        if short_host(target) == short_host(host):
            continue
        check_time("the remote holder checks")
        try:
            done = subprocess.run(
                ["ssh", "-o", "BatchMode=yes", "-o", "ConnectTimeout=2", target,
                 "omp-session-live", shlex.quote(holder)],
                capture_output=True, text=True, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                timeout=min(REMOTE_SECONDS, max(0.1, _RUN["deadline"] - time.time())),
            )
            if done.returncode == 0:
                checked[target] = "live omp"
                return target, checked
            checked[target] = "no live omp" if done.returncode == 1 else f"SSH/check failed ({done.returncode})"
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            checked[target] = "timed out"
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as error:
            checked[target] = f"SSH/check failed ({type(error).__name__})"
    return "", checked


def checked_hosts(host: str, remote: dict[str, str]) -> str:
    return "Hosts checked: " + ", ".join(
        [short_host(host)] + [name if result == "no live omp" else f"{name} ({result})"
                              for name, result in remote.items()]
    ) + "."

# --------------------------------------------------------------- this machine's omp processes


_HEADER_IDS: dict[tuple[int, int], str | None] = {}
_TRANSCRIPT_INDEX: tuple[dict[str, Path], list[Path]] | None = None
# Session id -> the transcript this watch last knew it by: every transcript an `omp` process
# holds open this run, every lookup that succeeded, and what the state file kept from earlier
# runs for the role holders and panes it still watches. `transcript_of` reads it first, so the
# session root is walked only for a session this watch never saw running.
_KNOWN_TRANSCRIPTS: dict[str, str] = {}
# The walk of the session root and the header pass behind it share ONE wall-clock budget per
# run. Unbounded, they were the whole of every run on 2026-11-02: at load 410-455 the box's root
# disk answered a walk of 19,287 transcripts in 247 s and the header pass in 325 s (cProfile,
# 13:06-13:16Z, at this unit's Nice and IO class), against the 25 s TimeoutStartSec it had, so from 09:00Z
# no run finished, and nothing after the first unseen holder -- the other roles, the pane check,
# every alert and clear -- ran at all. A walk that runs out is said to have run out.
TRANSCRIPT_WALK_SECONDS = 3.0
# A holder the walk missed is walked for again only this long after. The five holders unseen
# for hours on 2026-11-02 (alerted and escalated since 00:26Z) missed in every period and each
# period paid the whole budget for them; a session that comes back is seen by its process or
# its heartbeat first, so a later walk could only ever find an old file.
TRANSCRIPT_REWALK = 600.0
_TRANSCRIPT_WALK: dict = {"deadline": 0.0, "truncated": False}
# The session sweep of dispatch://OSSV-240 (`omp-session-sql-sweep.timer`) imports every
# transcript idle for 6 hours into the `omp_session_files` table on agents-postgres, then deletes
# the local file. The transcript of a holder that ended hours ago is therefore often not on this
# disk at all: bet-owner-nickmine's holder 01a19366 exited normally at 2026-11-02T05:41:32Z, the
# sweep imported its transcript at 14:08:13Z and deleted it at 14:10:26Z, and the 14:10:46Z run
# escalated a planned stop, because nothing it could read still held the exit. The sweep's own
# record (`deleted.tsv`) names each file it deleted; the tail is read back from the table.
SWEEP_STATE = Path.home() / ".local/share/omp-session-sql"
PSQL = ("psql", "-X", "-q", "-v", "ON_ERROR_STOP=1", "-At")
# One read of one row's last two 1 MiB parts took 0.18 s from this box (2026-11-02 14:20Z).
SWEEP_READ_SECONDS = 5.0
# Session id -> (path, swept at) for every top-level transcript the sweep deleted, read once per
# run and only when a holder's transcript is not on this disk.
_SWEEP_INDEX: dict[str, tuple[str, str]] | None = None
# Session id -> {"path", "swept_at", "mtime", "exited_at"}: what the table holds for a swept
# transcript. It is closed and never changes, so it is read once per holder and kept in the state
# file beside the known transcripts.
_SWEPT: dict[str, dict] = {}


# path -> [inode, session id]: the header of every transcript this watch looked up in its last
# run, kept in <state dir>/headers.json between runs. A header is written when the transcript is
# created and never rewritten, so (path, inode) names it for as long as the file lives. Without
# it every run re-read the headers of all ~500 transcripts the omp processes hold open: 38 s of a
# 39 s process scan at load 268, one read 5 s (2026-11-03 02:41Z, at this unit's Nice and IO
# class), and the slowest step of 49 of the 71 runs over 45 s in the six hours before.
_HEADER_CACHE: dict[str, list] = {}
# What the next run's cache will hold: only the transcripts looked up in this one, so the file
# never outgrows what omp holds open.
_HEADER_USED: dict[str, list] = {}


def load_header_cache(path: Path) -> None:
    _HEADER_CACHE.clear()
    _HEADER_USED.clear()
    try:
        loaded = json.loads(path.read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return
    if isinstance(loaded, dict):
        _HEADER_CACHE.update(
            {name: entry for name, entry in loaded.items() if isinstance(entry, list) and len(entry) == 2}
        )


def save_header_cache(path: Path) -> None:
    """Replaced whole, never fsynced: a lost cache costs one run its header reads and nothing else."""
    scratch = path.with_name(path.name + ".tmp")
    try:
        path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        scratch.write_text(json.dumps(_HEADER_USED, sort_keys=True))
        os.replace(scratch, path)
    except OSError as error:
        log(f"header cache {path} not written: {error}")


def header_known(path: str | Path, info: os.stat_result) -> tuple[bool, str | None]:
    """(True, id) when this run or the cache already holds the header of this file; else (False, None)."""
    key = (info.st_dev, info.st_ino)
    if key in _HEADER_IDS:
        return True, _HEADER_IDS[key]
    cached = _HEADER_CACHE.get(str(path))
    if cached and cached[0] == info.st_ino:
        _HEADER_IDS[key] = cached[1]
        _HEADER_USED[str(path)] = cached
        return True, cached[1]
    return False, None


def session_id_in_header(path: str | Path, info: os.stat_result | None = None, via: str = "") -> str | None:
    """The session id a transcript names in its own header, or None.

    A transcript's file name is not its identity, and neither is the command line that opened
    it. `omp --resume` takes a PATH as well as an id, and a lane that began as a subagent is
    resumed from a path named after the subagent (.../<stamp>_<parent id>/ArityLane.jsonl): no
    id on the command line, none in the file name, and a watch that reads only those two says a
    live session is gone. That is what happened at 2026-10-29 09:20:30Z on role bet64-eta-arity,
    whose holder was pid 724296, up 17,353 s, working with three subagents, and holding exactly
    such a file open.

    omp writes the id in a `{"type": "session", "id": ...}` record, which is not the first line:
    the first is the `title` record. So the first few records are read, not just one. Memoised on
    (device, inode), because a run reads the same transcript once per holding process, and kept
    across runs on (path, inode) in the header cache -- but only an answer that is final: a file
    whose session record is not written yet is read again by the next run. `via` is the
    /proc/<pid>/fd link the process scan found it by, so the file read is the file held open.
    """
    if info is None:
        try:
            info = os.stat(via or path)
        except OSError:
            return None
    known, found = header_known(path, info)
    if known:
        return found
    final = False
    try:
        with open(via or path, "rb") as handle:
            for _ in range(8):
                line = handle.readline(65536)
                if not line:
                    break
                try:
                    record = json.loads(line)
                except (ValueError, UnicodeDecodeError):
                    continue
                if not isinstance(record, dict) or record.get("type") != "session":
                    continue
                match = UUID_RE.search(str(record.get("id") or ""))
                if match:
                    found = match.group(0)
                final = True
                break
            else:
                # Eight records and none of them the session's: that is this file's answer.
                final = True
    except OSError:
        # A read that failed is not a file with no id in it. Caching it would make one transient
        # EMFILE or EACCES the answer for every later caller in this run.
        return None
    _HEADER_IDS[(info.st_dev, info.st_ino)] = found
    if final:
        _HEADER_USED[str(path)] = [info.st_ino, found]
    return found


# Command names whose processes may be an agent box's launcher (`agentbox new … -- omp --resume
# <id>`, run by bash; agentbox-run.ts, run by bun under the box's docker-init; the docker client).
BOX_LAUNCHER_COMMS = {"bash", "sh", "bun", "docker-init", "docker"}
# pid -> the launcher argv this run took a session from, so the process row can say that what
# holds the session is a box starting or running it, not an omp.
_BOX_LAUNCHERS: dict[int, str] = {}


def box_launcher_session(argv: list[str]) -> tuple[str | None, str | None]:
    """The session an agent box launcher is starting, from its own argv, else (None, None).

    `agentbox move` hangs a session's omp up and respawns its pane into `agentbox new … -- omp
    --resume <id>`; until the box is up and its omp has started, that omp is in no process, and
    on 2026-11-02 a respawn at 12:51:45Z had its box's omp start at 13:07:15Z, at load 410. The
    launcher processes live exactly as long as that box: they exit with its omp. So a launcher
    naming the session is a box starting or running it, and the watch counts it as present.

    Matched on argv WORDS, never on a substring of one: a pane's `bash -i -c '( setsid agentbox
    move --wake <id> … ); agentbox new … --resume <id>'` carries the whole command in one word
    and outlives a box that failed, so it is not a launcher.
    """
    names = [os.path.basename(word) for word in argv]
    launcher = "agentbox-run.ts" in names or any(
        name == "agentbox" and index + 1 < len(names) and names[index + 1] == "new"
        for index, name in enumerate(names)
    )
    if not launcher or "--resume" not in argv:
        return None, None
    return resume_target(" ".join(argv))


# The pane check's process table, from the same pass over /proc as the omp sessions: see
# `process_table`. None until this run's scan has filled it.
_PROC_TABLE: tuple[dict[int, list[int]], dict[int, tuple[str, int]]] | None = None
# What this run's process scan left undone: the transcript headers it had no time to read. A
# holder known only by one of them would look unseen, so `run_once` judges no unseen holder in a
# period that left any (see HEADER_READ_SECONDS).
_SCAN: dict = {"unread_headers": 0}


def read_stat(entry: str) -> tuple[str, int, int] | None:
    """(comm, ppid, tpgid) from /proc/<pid>/stat, or None when the process is gone."""
    try:
        with open(PROC_ROOT / entry / "stat", "rb") as handle:
            raw = handle.read().decode("utf8", "replace")
        head, _, tail = raw.rpartition(")")
        fields = tail.split()
        return head.partition("(")[2], int(fields[1]), int(fields[5])
    except (OSError, IndexError, ValueError):
        return None


# pid -> {"primary": the top-level session this omp runs, or None; "oneshot": started with -p}.
# The pane check reads it: a pane is remembered by the session its omp RUNS, never by a subagent
# transcript it holds open -- on 2026-11-03 pane %1740 was remembered as running 01a19ed8, an
# advisor subagent of 01a19e3c-8d64, so nothing about the move of 01a19e3c-8d64 could match it.
_OMP_PIDS: dict[int, dict] = {}


def opened_for_writing(fdinfo: Path) -> bool:
    """Whether the descriptor is open for writing: O_WRONLY or O_RDWR in the access mode of the
    octal `flags:` line of /proc/<pid>/fdinfo/<fd>. False when it cannot be read. The rule of
    omp-dup-watch (dotfiles 484c9c5): omp's session writer holds its own transcript
    O_WRONLY|O_APPEND (flags 02102001), and an omp opens OTHER sessions' transcripts read-only
    whenever a tool reads them."""
    try:
        text = fdinfo.read_text(errors="replace")
    except OSError:
        return False
    for line in text.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("flags:"):
            try:
                return int(line.split()[1], 8) & os.O_ACCMODE != os.O_RDONLY
            except (IndexError, ValueError):
                return False
    return False


def runs_no_session(argv: list[str]) -> bool:
    """True for an omp that runs no session of its own: a one-shot (`-p`/`--print`) or one
    started with `--no-session`. Its command line names whatever it was asked about, not a lane.
    On 2026-11-03 06:48-07:13Z the skill librarian's annotation batch ran
    `omp -p ... --no-session ... @…/known/runs/opus48/01a19366-…-s20/task.md` over session
    01a19366, and this watch read each of those four processes as 01a19366 running elsewhere."""
    return any(word in ("-p", "--print", "--no-session") for word in argv[1:])


def top_level_session(path: str) -> str | None:
    """The session id of a top-level transcript (<session root>/<dir>/<stamp>_<id>.jsonl), or
    None for anything else: a subagent's file lives one directory deeper, under its parent's."""
    candidate = Path(path)
    if candidate.parent.parent != SESSION_ROOT:
        return None
    match = re.fullmatch(r"[0-9T:.-]+Z_(" + UUID_RE.pattern + r")\.jsonl", candidate.name)
    return match.group(1) if match else None


@bounded_step("the process scan", whole_budget=True)
def omp_sessions_by_pid() -> dict[str, int]:
    """Every session id an `omp` process on this machine holds, mapped to that pid.

    Three sources, and only these: the `--resume`/`--restore` target (`omp --resume <id>`, or
    the header of the file `omp --resume <path>` names), the file name of a transcript the omp
    holds open FOR WRITING (`opened_for_writing`), and that transcript's own header. Never other
    words of the command line, never a transcript it only reads, and nothing at all from an omp
    that runs no session of its own (`runs_no_session`). The header is what makes a resumed
    subagent lane visible; without it the process holds the session and nothing can tell.

    An omp in an agent box is a host process too: its comm is `omp` and its command line is
    readable, but its uid is shifted (166536 here) and its /proc/<pid>/fd and environ are not,
    so for a boxed omp the command line is the only one of the three. Then, for the sessions no
    omp names, an agent box launcher that names it: see `box_launcher_session`.

    A pid here is positive evidence of life and nothing else. Its absence is not evidence of
    death: this reads only processes whose comm is `omp` or a launcher's, so a lane under a
    differently named parent is invisible to it, which is why `transcript_holders` reads the
    transcript's own openers before anything alerts.

    ONE pass over /proc per run: /proc/<pid>/stat of every process gives the comm this filters
    on and the parent and terminal group the pane check needs (`_PROC_TABLE`), where two passes
    read 3,400 comm files and 3,400 stat files. Headers come from the header cache first; the
    ones not in it are read inside HEADER_READ_SECONDS, and the rest are counted in
    `_SCAN["unread_headers"]` for the next run to read.
    """
    global _PROC_TABLE
    held: dict[str, int] = {}
    launchers: dict[str, tuple[int, str]] = {}
    children: dict[int, list[int]] = {}
    info: dict[int, tuple[str, int]] = {}
    _BOX_LAUNCHERS.clear()
    _OMP_PIDS.clear()
    _SCAN["unread_headers"] = 0
    header_deadline = min(time.time() + HEADER_READ_SECONDS, _RUN["deadline"])
    for entry in os.listdir(PROC_ROOT):
        if not entry.isdigit():
            continue
        check_time("the process scan")
        stat = read_stat(entry)
        if stat is None:
            continue
        pid = int(entry)
        comm, ppid, tpgid = stat
        children.setdefault(ppid, []).append(pid)
        info[pid] = (comm, tpgid)
        if comm != "omp" and comm not in BOX_LAUNCHER_COMMS:
            continue
        try:
            with open(PROC_ROOT / entry / "cmdline", "rb") as handle:
                argv = handle.read().decode("utf8", "replace").split("\0")
        except OSError:
            continue
        argv = [word for word in argv if word]
        if comm != "omp":
            session, path = box_launcher_session(argv)
            if session:
                launchers.setdefault(session, (pid, " ".join(argv)))
                if path:
                    _KNOWN_TRANSCRIPTS[session] = path
            continue
        # An omp that runs no session of its own names none: its command line is about
        # whatever it was asked, and NICKMINE's id in an annotation batch's task path read as
        # NICKMINE running in four processes on 2026-11-03 (see runs_no_session).
        oneshot = runs_no_session(argv)
        _OMP_PIDS[pid] = {"primary": None, "oneshot": oneshot}
        if oneshot:
            continue
        # A session id comes from three places only: the `--resume`/`--restore` target, a
        # transcript this omp holds open FOR WRITING, and that transcript's own header. Never
        # from other words of the command line, and never from a transcript it only reads.
        found: set[str] = set()
        resumed, path = resume_target(" ".join(argv))
        if resumed:
            found.add(resumed)
            if path:
                _KNOWN_TRANSCRIPTS[resumed] = path
        tops: list[str] = []
        try:
            descriptors = os.listdir(PROC_ROOT / str(pid) / "fd")
        except OSError:
            descriptors = []
        for descriptor in descriptors:
            link = f"{PROC_ROOT}/{pid}/fd/{descriptor}"
            try:
                target = os.readlink(link)
            except OSError:
                continue
            if "/.omp/agent/sessions/" not in target or not target.endswith(".jsonl"):
                continue
            if not opened_for_writing(PROC_ROOT / str(pid) / "fdinfo" / descriptor):
                continue
            named = UUID_RE.findall(os.path.basename(target))
            try:
                opened = os.stat(link)
            except OSError:
                continue
            known, from_header = header_known(target, opened)
            if not known:
                if time.time() <= header_deadline:
                    from_header = session_id_in_header(target, opened, via=link)
                else:
                    _SCAN["unread_headers"] += 1
            # The file a running omp writes is the transcript of these ids, for certain: kept,
            # so the lookup after this holder stops needs no walk of the session root.
            for session_id in named + ([from_header] if from_header else []):
                _KNOWN_TRANSCRIPTS[session_id] = target
            found.update(named)
            if from_header:
                found.add(from_header)
            top = top_level_session(target)
            if top:
                tops.append(top)
        for session_id in found:
            held.setdefault(session_id, pid)
        _OMP_PIDS[pid]["primary"] = resumed or (sorted(set(tops))[0] if len(set(tops)) == 1 else None)
    for session, (pid, command) in launchers.items():
        if session not in held:
            held[session] = pid
            _BOX_LAUNCHERS[pid] = command
    _PROC_TABLE = (children, info)
    return held


# (device, inode) of every open .jsonl -> the pids holding it, read once per run on first use.
_OPEN_JSONL: dict[tuple[int, int], list[int]] | None = None


@bounded_step("the open-file scan")
def transcript_holders(path: str | Path) -> list[int]:
    """Every pid on this machine with `path` open FOR WRITING, whatever the process is called.

    `omp_sessions_by_pid` reads only processes whose comm is `omp`. This reads the file's own
    openers instead, by device and inode, so a lane running under a wrapper, a renamed binary or
    a box's init still counts as a holder. Only a writer counts (`opened_for_writing`): a tool
    that reads another session's transcript holds it open read-only, and is not that session.
    It reads every descriptor of every process this user can read, so it does that ONCE per
    run, for every holder asked about: once per holder was 0.66 s each at load 410 on
    2026-11-02, with six open incidents in every period. A scan the run's budget stops leaves no
    table behind: half a table would say "nobody holds it".
    """
    global _OPEN_JSONL
    try:
        info = os.stat(path)
    except OSError:
        return []
    if _OPEN_JSONL is None:
        table: dict[tuple[int, int], list[int]] = {}
        for entry in os.listdir(PROC_ROOT):
            if not entry.isdigit():
                continue
            check_time("the open-file scan")
            directory = f"{PROC_ROOT}/{entry}/fd"
            try:
                descriptors = os.listdir(directory)
            except OSError:
                continue
            for descriptor in descriptors:
                try:
                    if not os.readlink(f"{directory}/{descriptor}").endswith(".jsonl"):
                        continue
                    if not opened_for_writing(PROC_ROOT / entry / "fdinfo" / descriptor):
                        continue
                    seen = os.stat(f"{directory}/{descriptor}")
                except OSError:
                    continue
                pids = table.setdefault((seen.st_dev, seen.st_ino), [])
                if int(entry) not in pids:
                    pids.append(int(entry))
        _OPEN_JSONL = table
    return list(_OPEN_JSONL.get((info.st_dev, info.st_ino), []))


def transcript_index() -> tuple[dict[str, Path], list[Path]]:
    """One walk of the session root per run: the id index, and the headers left to read.

    Returns ({session id taken from a file NAME: its newest transcript}, [transcripts whose
    name carries no id, newest first]).

    One walk, not one per role. `SESSION_ROOT.glob("**/*_<id>.jsonl")` costs 0.65 s over this
    box's 34,238 transcripts, and a run that called it once per role spent 19 s of its 25 s
    TimeoutStartSec on it (measured 2026-10-29 at load 77); a run systemd SIGTERMs is a watch
    that has stopped watching while its timer still looks healthy.

    And bounded by TRANSCRIPT_WALK_SECONDS, which it shares with the header pass in
    `transcript_of`. The working directories are walked newest first, so a budget that runs out
    leaves out the oldest sessions; a walk that stops sets `_TRANSCRIPT_WALK["truncated"]`, and
    the transcript row says so.
    """
    global _TRANSCRIPT_INDEX
    if _TRANSCRIPT_INDEX is not None:
        return _TRANSCRIPT_INDEX
    deadline = min(time.time() + TRANSCRIPT_WALK_SECONDS, _RUN["deadline"])
    _TRANSCRIPT_WALK.update(deadline=deadline, truncated=False)
    cutoff = time.time() - 7 * 86400
    by_id: dict[str, tuple[float, Path]] = {}
    unnamed: list[tuple[float, Path]] = []
    tops: list[tuple[float, str]] = []

    def out_of_time() -> bool:
        if time.time() > deadline:
            _TRANSCRIPT_WALK["truncated"] = True
        return _TRANSCRIPT_WALK["truncated"]

    try:
        with os.scandir(SESSION_ROOT) as entries:
            for entry in entries:
                if out_of_time():
                    break
                try:
                    if entry.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False):
                        tops.append((entry.stat(follow_symlinks=False).st_mtime, entry.path))
                except OSError:
                    continue
    except OSError:
        pass
    tops.sort(key=lambda item: -item[0])
    walks = (os.walk(top, onerror=lambda _error: None) for _, top in tops)
    for root, _, names in (step for walk in walks for step in walk):
        if out_of_time():
            break
        for name in names:
            if out_of_time():
                break
            if not name.endswith(".jsonl"):
                continue
            candidate = os.path.join(root, name)
            try:
                stamp = os.stat(candidate).st_mtime
            except OSError:
                continue
            match = UUID_RE.search(name)
            if match:
                best = by_id.get(match.group(0))
                if best is None or stamp > best[0]:
                    by_id[match.group(0)] = (stamp, Path(candidate))
            elif stamp >= cutoff:
                # Only these need their headers read, and only when the index misses.
                unnamed.append((stamp, Path(candidate)))
    unnamed.sort(key=lambda item: -item[0])
    _TRANSCRIPT_INDEX = (
        {session: path for session, (_, path) in by_id.items()},
        [path for _, path in unnamed],
    )
    return _TRANSCRIPT_INDEX


def transcript_in_dir(directory: Path, session_id: str) -> Path | None:
    """The newest `<stamp>_<id>.jsonl` directly inside one session directory: one listing.

    omp writes a session's transcript into the session directory of its working directory, and
    Envoy lists that working directory for every role holder, so this is where a holder's
    transcript is. On 2026-11-02 at 13:20:57Z the walk of the whole session root ran out of its
    3 s budget before it reached bet-owner-nickmine's holder, whose transcript sat in exactly
    this directory (a symlink into /mnt/durable, where omp-transcript-offload had moved it at
    07:50:22Z), and the watch sent a first alert about a holder that had exited normally at
    05:41:32Z. A symlink counts: `stat` follows it, as omp does.
    """
    suffix = f"_{session_id}.jsonl"
    best: tuple[float, Path] | None = None
    try:
        with os.scandir(directory) as entries:
            for entry in entries:
                if not entry.name.endswith(suffix):
                    continue
                try:
                    stamp = entry.stat().st_mtime
                except OSError:
                    continue
                if best is None or stamp > best[0]:
                    best = (stamp, Path(entry.path))
    except OSError:
        return None
    return best[1] if best else None


@bounded_step("the transcript lookup")
def transcript_of(session_id: str, walk: bool = True, directory: str = "") -> Path | None:
    """The session's own transcript file, the newest one when a session was resumed.

    First the transcript this watch already knows the session by (`_KNOWN_TRANSCRIPTS`): the
    file an omp process holds open for it now or held in an earlier run. That is the common
    case and costs one stat; a holder this watch saw running never needs the walk below.

    Then two passes, for the same reason `session_id_in_header` exists: a file named
    `<stamp>_<id>` is found anywhere under the session root, including the subagent
    directories that `SESSION_ROOT.iterdir()` alone never descended into, and a file whose name
    carries no id at all (ArityLane.jsonl) is found by reading headers, newest first. That
    second pass is not cheap and not rare: 25,936 of this box's 34,272 transcripts carry no id
    in their name (every subagent's does not). Both passes run only on a miss, once per run,
    inside TRANSCRIPT_WALK_SECONDS together, which is what keeps a whole period inside its
    TimeoutStartSec at any load. Before them, the session directory of the holder's own
    working directory (`transcript_in_dir`), which costs one listing and so runs every time.
    `walk=False` stops before the walk: a holder whose last walk missed is walked again only
    every TRANSCRIPT_REWALK seconds (see run_once).
    """
    if not UUID_RE.fullmatch(session_id or ""):
        return None
    known = _KNOWN_TRANSCRIPTS.get(session_id)
    if known and os.path.isfile(known):
        return Path(known)
    if directory:
        found = transcript_in_dir(session_dir_of(directory), session_id)
        if found:
            _KNOWN_TRANSCRIPTS[session_id] = str(found)
            return found
    if not walk:
        return None
    by_id, unnamed = transcript_index()
    found = by_id.get(session_id)
    if not found:
        for candidate in unnamed:
            if time.time() > _TRANSCRIPT_WALK["deadline"]:
                _TRANSCRIPT_WALK["truncated"] = True
                break
            if session_id_in_header(candidate) == session_id:
                found = candidate
                break
    if found:
        _KNOWN_TRANSCRIPTS[session_id] = str(found)
    return found


@bounded_step("the transcript tail read")
def normal_exit_at(path: str | Path | None) -> str | None:
    """When the transcript ends in a normal session exit, the time it records; else None.

    omp ends a transcript it closes on purpose with
    {"type":"custom","customType":"session_exit","data":{"reason":"dispose","kind":"normal",
    "recordedAt":"2026-11-01T22:46:26.874Z"},...}. On 2026-11-01 sessions 01a1798a-9911
    ("Scan and redact leaked API keys") and 01a1798a-995f ("SCHED") in ~/src/machcsl-riscv
    exited that way at 22:46:26Z and 22:46:56Z still holding roles, and this watch alerted and
    escalated on both as if they had stopped. The exit counts when nothing follows it, or when
    every entry after it is dated within EXIT_TAIL_S of it, before or after: omp can flush the
    turn in flight after the exit marker. A session resumed after an exit writes minutes
    past it, and any other end -- a crash, a kill, a hang -- is still an incident.
    """
    if not path:
        return None
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as handle:
            handle.seek(0, os.SEEK_END)
            size = handle.tell()
            handle.seek(max(0, size - 65536))
            lines = handle.read().splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return None
    return exit_in_lines(lines)


@bounded_step("a transcript's stat")
def file_state(path: str | Path) -> tuple[bool, float | None]:
    """(whether `path` exists, its mtime or None when that cannot be read), through a symlink."""
    if not os.path.exists(path):
        return False, None
    try:
        return True, os.path.getmtime(path)
    except OSError:
        return True, None


@bounded_step("a transcript's session record")
def session_cwd(path: str | Path) -> str:
    """The working directory a transcript's `session` record names, or "" when none of its first
    three lines is one or the file cannot be read."""
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as handle:
            for _ in range(3):
                entry = json.loads(handle.readline(65536) or b"{}")
                if entry.get("type") == "session":
                    return entry.get("cwd") or ""
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        pass
    return ""



def exit_in_lines(lines: list[bytes]) -> str | None:
    """`normal_exit_at` on the last lines of a transcript, wherever they were read from."""
    tail: list[dict] = []
    for line in reversed(lines):
        if not line.strip():
            continue
        try:
            entry = json.loads(line)
        except ValueError:
            return None
        if not isinstance(entry, dict):
            return None
        data = entry.get("data")
        if (
            entry.get("type") == "custom"
            and entry.get("customType") == "session_exit"
            and isinstance(data, dict)
            and data.get("kind") == "normal"
        ):
            recorded = str(data.get("recordedAt") or entry.get("timestamp") or "")
            if tail:
                exit_at = _entry_time(recorded)
                if exit_at is None or any(
                    abs((_entry_time(str(later.get("timestamp") or "")) or float("inf")) - exit_at) > EXIT_TAIL_S
                    for later in tail
                ):
                    return None
            return recorded or "unknown time"
        tail.append(entry)
        if len(tail) > EXIT_TAIL_ENTRIES:
            return None
    return None


def swept_index() -> dict[str, tuple[str, str]]:
    """Session id -> (path, deleted at) for each top-level transcript the session sweep deleted.

    Read from the sweep's own record, `deleted.tsv` (path, bytes, sha256, symlink target, deleted
    at), once per run and only when a holder's transcript is not on this disk. The last row for
    an id wins. A subagent's transcript (`<stamp>_<id>/Sub.jsonl`) is not the session's.
    """
    global _SWEEP_INDEX
    if _SWEEP_INDEX is not None:
        return _SWEEP_INDEX
    _SWEEP_INDEX = {}
    try:
        with open(SWEEP_STATE / "deleted.tsv", "rb") as handle:
            for raw in handle:
                fields = raw.decode("utf-8", "replace").rstrip("\n").split("\t")
                name = os.path.basename(fields[0])
                match = re.fullmatch(r"[0-9T:.-]+Z_(" + UUID_RE.pattern + r")\.jsonl", name)
                if match:
                    _SWEEP_INDEX[match.group(1)] = (fields[0], fields[4] if len(fields) > 4 else "")
    except OSError:
        pass
    return _SWEEP_INDEX


@bounded_step("the session sweep's record")
def swept_transcript(session_id: str) -> dict | None:
    """What the session sweep did with this session's transcript, or None when it did not take it.

    {"path", "swept_at", "mtime", "exited_at"} once the table has been read, kept in `_SWEPT`;
    with "error" instead when the read failed, which is not kept, so the next run reads again.
    The row is keyed by the file's full path today, and by its path under ~/.omp/agent
    (`sessions/...`) after the key migration dispatch://INFRA-997 plans, so both are asked for.
    """
    if session_id in _SWEPT:
        return _SWEPT[session_id]
    entry = swept_index().get(session_id)
    if not entry:
        return None
    path, swept_at = entry
    record = {"path": path, "swept_at": swept_at, "mtime": None, "exited_at": None}
    keys = [path]
    agent_root = str(SESSION_ROOT.parent) + "/"
    if path.startswith(agent_root):
        keys.append(path[len(agent_root):])
    listed = ", ".join("'" + key.replace("'", "''") + "'" for key in keys)
    query = (
        "SELECT coalesce(f.mtime_ms::text, '') || E'\\n' || coalesce((SELECT right(string_agg("
        "t.content, '' ORDER BY t.start_offset), 65536) FROM (SELECT p.content, p.start_offset"
        " FROM omp_session_files_parts p WHERE p.path = f.path ORDER BY p.start_offset DESC"
        f" LIMIT 2) t), right(f.content, 65536), '') FROM omp_session_files f WHERE f.path IN ({listed})"
        " LIMIT 1;\n"
    )
    try:
        dsn = (SWEEP_STATE / "dsn").read_text().strip()
        done = subprocess.run(
            [*PSQL, dsn, "-f", "-"], input=query, capture_output=True, text=True,
            timeout=SWEEP_READ_SECONDS,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as error:
        return {**record, "error": f"the table could not be read: {error}"}
    if done.returncode != 0 or not done.stdout.strip():
        problem = done.stderr.strip().splitlines()[-1:] or ["no row for it in omp_session_files"]
        return {**record, "error": f"the table could not be read: {problem[0][:200]}"}
    stamp, _, tail = done.stdout.partition("\n")
    record["mtime"] = int(stamp) / 1000 if stamp.strip().isdigit() else None
    record["exited_at"] = exit_in_lines(tail.encode().splitlines())
    _SWEPT[session_id] = record
    return record


# How long after a normal exit omp may still write the turn it had in flight, and how many such
# entries are looked through, before the exit no longer counts as the transcript's end.
EXIT_TAIL_S = 10.0
EXIT_TAIL_ENTRIES = 20


def _entry_time(stamp: str) -> float | None:
    """A transcript timestamp (`2026-11-02T07:30:34.078Z`) as a UTC epoch, or None when it has none."""
    match = re.fullmatch(r"(\d{4}-\d\d-\d\dT\d\d:\d\d:\d\d)(\.\d+)?Z", stamp)
    if not match:
        return None
    return epoch_of(match.group(1) + "Z") + float(match.group(2) or 0)


# --------------------------------------------------- what this run could establish about a holder


@bounded_step("the working-directory walk")
def newest_write(directory: str, holder: str = "", now: float | None = None) -> dict:
    """The newest regular file under a holder's working directory, named and dated.

    Returns {"dir", "file", "mtime", "age", "scanned", "truncated", "error", "transcript",
    "transcript_mtime"}. `file` is always the path of the file whose mtime `mtime` is, because
    "something is writing in there" is not a finding a reader can judge and "ps.log was written
    1 s ago while R-output.txt has not moved since 22:18:55Z" is.

    `transcript` is a .jsonl under this directory whose NAME carries the holder's id. A lane
    whose harness redirects the session directory (PI_CODING_AGENT_SESSION_DIR) writes the
    holder's own transcript there and nothing under ~/.omp/agent/sessions, which is why the
    transcript source saw nothing for both 2026-10-30 incidents while the file sat in
    <run>/R/<stamp>_<holder>.jsonl. That file IS the session's own transcript, so its freshness
    is a sighting in the same sense as the session root's -- unlike every other file here.

    Bounded by DIR_SCAN_ENTRIES, DIR_SCAN_DEPTH and DIR_SCAN_SECONDS, and it never follows a
    symlinked directory. A walk that hit a bound sets `truncated`, and a truncated walk is
    never read as "nothing recent": the newest file may be the one it did not reach.
    """
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    found: dict = {
        "dir": directory, "file": "", "mtime": None, "age": None, "scanned": 0,
        "truncated": False, "error": "", "transcript": "", "transcript_mtime": None,
    }
    if not directory:
        found["error"] = "no working directory is recorded for this holder"
        return found
    # The budget runs from NOW, not from `now`: `now` is the run's start clock, shared by every
    # measurement in a period so that two roles cannot date the same file differently, and a
    # run that has already spent six seconds on Envoy and /proc would otherwise hand this walk
    # a deadline in the past. It did, on the first live run of this step at 2026-10-30 23:06Z:
    # the walk stopped after ONE entry and reported the lane's directory as unreadable.
    deadline = min(time.time() + DIR_SCAN_SECONDS, _RUN["deadline"])
    stack = [(directory, 0)]
    while stack and not found["truncated"]:
        path, depth = stack.pop()
        try:
            entries = list(os.scandir(path))
        except OSError as error:
            if path == directory:
                found["error"] = f"{directory}: {error.strerror or error}"
            continue
        for entry in entries:
            found["scanned"] += 1
            if found["scanned"] > DIR_SCAN_ENTRIES or time.time() > deadline:
                found["truncated"] = True
                break
            try:
                if entry.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False):
                    if depth < DIR_SCAN_DEPTH:
                        stack.append((entry.path, depth + 1))
                    continue
                if not entry.is_file(follow_symlinks=False):
                    continue
                stamp = entry.stat(follow_symlinks=False).st_mtime
            except OSError:
                continue
            if found["mtime"] is None or stamp > found["mtime"]:
                found["mtime"], found["file"] = stamp, entry.path
            if (
                holder
                and entry.name.endswith(".jsonl")
                and holder in entry.name
                and (found["transcript_mtime"] is None or stamp > found["transcript_mtime"])
            ):
                found["transcript_mtime"], found["transcript"] = stamp, entry.path
    if found["mtime"] is not None:
        found["age"] = now - found["mtime"]
    return found


def holder_evidence(
    envoy: Envoy,
    holder: str,
    held: dict[str, int],
    live: dict[str, dict],
    write_grace: float,
    now: float,
    directory: str = "",
    dir_grace: float = DEFAULT_DIR_GRACE,
    walk: bool = True,
    deep: bool = True,
    registry_result: tuple[int, dict] | None = None,
) -> dict:
    """Every source this watch has on one holder, and what each one answered.

    Four sources, each of which can say "found" and none of which can say "gone":

      process    an `omp` process naming the session on its command line, in an open
                 transcript's file name, or in that transcript's header
      transcript how long ago the session's transcript was last written
      registry   Envoy's own entry and heartbeat, read with GET /v1/sessions and confirmed for
                 an absent holder with GET /v1/registry/<id>; never with GET /v1/roles/<role>,
                 which releases the claim of exactly the holder this watch cannot find
      openers    any process at all holding the session's transcript open. This one stats every
                 descriptor of every readable process (0.17 s here, 2,584 processes), so it is
                 read ONLY when the first three came back silent -- which is also exactly when
                 it can change the answer. A healthy role never pays for it, and an open
                 incident still pays for it every period, because it is the one source that can
                 see a lane running under a name this watch does not know and so the one that
                 can clear such an incident.
      directory  the newest regular file under the holder's WORKING DIRECTORY, named and
                 dated. It is never a sighting -- `seen` is False on this row whatever it
                 finds -- and it never licenses a sentence about the lane working. It is read
                 on the same gate as `openers`, and it carries `dir_fresh`, which is what
                 quiets an incident nothing else can ever clear. See DEFAULT_DIR_GRACE.

    `alive` is true when ANY source found it. The verdict when none did is CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER,
    and there is no path to HOLDER_DEAD: see that constant.

    `deep=False` reads only the two free sources and the transcript this watch already knows:
    no openers scan, no working-directory walk, no read of the session sweep's table. It is for
    a holder whose incident is finished -- a recorded normal exit, a quieted alert -- where the
    only question left is whether the session came back, which a process or a heartbeat answers.
    On 2026-11-03 00:18:13Z-00:19:33Z one such holder cost a run 80 s of its 90 s and the run
    was killed.

    """
    sources: list[tuple[str, bool, str]] = []

    pid = held.get(holder)
    sources.append(
        ("process", pid is not None,
         (f"an agent box is starting or running this session: launcher pid {pid}"
          f" ({_BOX_LAUNCHERS[pid][:200]}); its omp is not up yet, or is in a box whose omp this"
          " watch cannot name" if pid in _BOX_LAUNCHERS
          else f"an omp process holds this session: pid {pid}") if pid
         else f"{CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER} in any omp process on this machine (no command line names it,"
              " no open transcript's name or header does, and no agent box launcher names it)")
    )

    entry = live.get(holder)
    heartbeat = (entry or {}).get("last_seen")
    heartbeat = heartbeat / 1000 if isinstance(heartbeat, (int, float)) and heartbeat else None
    if entry is not None:
        # A registration is not a sighting; a recent HEARTBEAT is, because a process that has
        # stopped cannot send one. Envoy keeps an entry for its whole 300 s TTL after the last
        # beat, and that stale window is exactly the one the 2026-10-27T05:52Z loss hid in: the
        # role looked deliverable for five minutes after the session stopped. Measured on this
        # box, 610 live sessions beat at a median age of 43 s and p90 86 s, so the same
        # --write-grace window (120 s) separates a beating session from a stale entry, and a
        # session that beats more slowly than that is covered by the other three sources.
        fresh = heartbeat is not None and now - heartbeat <= write_grace
        registry_state = "live" if fresh else "stale"
        sources.append(
            ("registry", fresh,
             f"Envoy heard from this session {human(now - heartbeat)} ago"
             if fresh else
             (f"Envoy still lists this session, but its last heartbeat was"
              f" {human(now - heartbeat)} ago, outside the {int(write_grace)} s window: a"
              " registration outlives its holder by up to the 300 s TTL, so the entry alone is"
              " not a sighting"
              if heartbeat else
              "Envoy lists this session but records no heartbeat time, so the entry is not a"
              " sighting"))
        )
    else:
        status, body = registry_result if registry_result is not None else envoy.registry(holder)
        if status == 404:
            registry_state = "absent"
            sources.append(
                ("registry", False,
                 f"{CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER} in Envoy's registry: absent from GET /v1/sessions, and"
                 " GET /v1/registry/<holder> answered 404. Envoy drops a live session that stops"
                 " heartbeating inside its 300 s TTL, which a loaded box does, so this says"
                 " nothing about the process")
            )
        elif status == 200:
            beat = body.get("last_seen")
            beat = beat / 1000 if isinstance(beat, (int, float)) and beat else None
            heartbeat = beat
            fresh = beat is not None and now - beat <= write_grace
            registry_state = "live" if fresh else "stale"
            sources.append(
                ("registry", fresh,
                 "Envoy's registry holds this session and heard from it"
                 f" {human(now - beat)} ago (GET /v1/registry/<holder> answered 200); the"
                 " /v1/sessions list this run read did not hold it"
                 if fresh else
                 "Envoy's registry holds this session (GET /v1/registry/<holder> answered 200)"
                 f" but its last heartbeat was {human(now - beat) if beat else 'never recorded'},"
                 " which is not a sighting")
            )
        else:
            registry_state = "unreadable"
            sources.append(
                ("registry", False,
                 f"Envoy's registry could not be read: GET /v1/registry/<holder> answered"
                 f" {status} {body.get('error') or ''}".strip())
            )

    # The transcript is looked up only when the two cheap sources are silent. A holder a process
    # or a fresh heartbeat already shows is found, and its transcript changes nothing that is
    # said about it; looking it up for every role every period is what put the session-root walk
    # into every run of 2026-11-02 (see TRANSCRIPT_WALK_SECONDS).
    seen_already = any(seen for _, seen, _ in sources)
    transcript = None if seen_already else transcript_of(holder, walk and deep, directory)
    last_write = file_state(transcript)[1] if transcript else None

    # The working-directory walk, on the same gate as `openers`: the first two sources are
    # silent and the session root holds nothing fresh for this holder. A healthy role never
    # pays for it, and a role whose transcript is being written right now does not either.
    scan = {"dir": directory, "file": "", "mtime": None, "age": None, "scanned": 0,
            "truncated": False, "error": "not read: a source above already found the holder",
            "transcript": "", "transcript_mtime": None}
    if deep and not any(seen for _, seen, _ in sources) and not (
        last_write and now - last_write <= write_grace
    ):
        scan = newest_write(directory, holder, now)
        if transcript is None and scan["transcript"]:
            # The holder's own transcript, written where its harness put it instead of the
            # session root. Same file, same meaning: see newest_write.
            transcript = Path(scan["transcript"])
            last_write = scan["transcript_mtime"]

    # On no disk here at all: the session sweep may have moved it into Postgres (see SWEEP_STATE).
    swept = None if seen_already or transcript or not deep else swept_transcript(holder)
    if swept and swept["mtime"]:
        last_write = swept["mtime"]

    where = (
        f"{transcript} (in the holder's own working directory, not the session root)"
        if transcript and scan["transcript"] and str(transcript) == scan["transcript"]
        else str(transcript)
    )
    if seen_already:
        sources.append(
            ("transcript", False, "not read: a source above already found the holder")
        )
    elif swept:
        sources.append(
            ("transcript", False,
             f"its transcript {swept['path']} is no longer on this disk: the session sweep"
             " (dispatch://OSSV-240) moved it into Postgres omp_sessions and deleted it at"
             f" {swept['swept_at'] or 'a time it did not record'}; "
             + (swept["error"] if swept.get("error") else
                f"it was last written {iso(swept['mtime'])}, and "
                + (f"its last entry is a normal session exit at {swept['exited_at']}"
                   if swept["exited_at"] else "its last entry is not a normal session exit")))
        )
    elif last_write and now - last_write <= write_grace:
        sources.append(
            ("transcript", True,
             f"its transcript {where} was written {human(now - last_write)} ago, inside the"
             f" {int(write_grace)} s window: something is still writing this session")
        )
    elif last_write:
        sources.append(
            ("transcript", False,
             f"its transcript {where} was last written {human(now - last_write)} ago"
             f" ({iso(last_write)}), outside the {int(write_grace)} s window -- which a session"
             " waiting on a model or a long tool call also looks like")
        )
    else:
        sources.append(
            ("transcript", False,
             f"{CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER} in any transcript under {SESSION_ROOT}, by file name or"
             " header, nor in any .jsonl named after it under "
             + (directory or "(no working directory recorded)")
             + (f"; {SESSION_ROOT} was searched for it less than {int(TRANSCRIPT_REWALK)} s ago"
                " and held nothing this watch reached, so it was not searched again this period"
                if not walk else
                f"; the search of {SESSION_ROOT} stopped at its {TRANSCRIPT_WALK_SECONDS:g} s"
                " budget, so a transcript in a part it did not reach was not seen"
                if _TRANSCRIPT_WALK["truncated"] else ""))
        )

    openers: list[int] = []
    if deep and not any(seen for _, seen, _ in sources):
        if transcript:
            openers = transcript_holders(transcript)
            sources.append(
                ("openers", bool(openers),
                 f"its transcript is open for writing in pid(s) {', '.join(str(p) for p in openers)}"
                 if openers
                 else "no process THIS USER CAN READ holds its transcript open for writing,"
                      " whatever the process is called (a reader is not the session) -- a lane"
                      " inside an agent box has a uid-shifted /proc and is invisible here"
                 )
            )
        else:
            # Said out loud rather than left out: three rows and four sources would read as a
            # source that answered nothing, when in fact there was no file to look at.
            sources.append(
                ("openers", False,
                 "not looked at: its transcript is a row in Postgres, which no process here can"
                 " hold open" if swept else
                 "not looked at: there is no transcript to find openers of")
            )

    # The fifth row. `seen` is False on it however recent the file is: this says what was
    # written in the lane's directory, never that the lane works or that the holder lives.
    dir_fresh = (
        scan["age"] is not None and scan["age"] <= dir_grace and not scan["truncated"]
    )
    if scan["error"] and scan["mtime"] is None:
        dir_sentence = f"{scan['error']}, so there is no write to date"
    elif scan["mtime"] is None:
        dir_sentence = (
            f"nothing under {directory} was readable as a file"
            + (f" (the walk stopped early after {scan['scanned']} entries)" if scan["truncated"]
               else f" ({scan['scanned']} entries walked)")
        )
    else:
        dir_sentence = (
            f"the newest file under {directory} is {scan['file']}, written {iso(scan['mtime'])}"
            f" ({human(scan['age'])} ago; {scan['scanned']} entries walked"
            + (", WALK TRUNCATED so a newer file may exist beyond it" if scan["truncated"] else "")
            + "). This says that file was written. It does NOT say the lane is working: on"
            " 2026-10-30 ps.log in such a directory grew about a kilobyte a second while the"
            " probe's own outputs had stopped 3 minutes earlier"
        )
    sources.append(("directory", False, dir_sentence))

    found = next((sentence for _, seen, sentence in sources if seen), "")
    found_source = next((name for name, seen, _ in sources if seen), "")
    return {
        "alive": bool(found),
        "found": found,
        "pid": pid or (openers[0] if openers else None),
        "sources": sources,
        "registry": registry_state,
        "heartbeat": heartbeat,
        "transcript": str(transcript or ""),
        "last_write": last_write,
        "verdict": found or CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER,
        "dir_fresh": dir_fresh,
        "dir_file": scan["file"],
        "dir_write": scan["mtime"],
        "dir_sentence": dir_sentence,
        "dir_truncated": scan["truncated"],
        "found_source": found_source,
        # Read whenever a transcript was found at all: with deep=False that is only one this
        # watch already knew the path of, which costs a stat and a 64 KiB tail read, and it is
        # what tells a planned stop from a lane that was resumed after it.
        "exited_at": None if found else (
            normal_exit_at(transcript) if transcript else (swept or {}).get("exited_at")
        ),
        # What the session sweep did with the transcript, when it is not on this disk.
        "swept": swept,
        # True when this call walked the session root for the holder and found nothing.
        "walk_missed": walk and deep and not seen_already and not transcript and not swept,
    }


def guard_verdict(text: str) -> str:
    """Refuse to send a message that claims a holder is dead.

    Nothing in this file establishes that, so a message that says it is a defect, not a finding.
    Cheap, and it is the one rule the 2026-10-29 incident turned on.
    """
    if HOLDER_DEAD in text:
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"refusing to send a message that claims {HOLDER_DEAD!r}: this watch cannot"
            " establish that"
        )
    return text


@bounded_step("the handover-file search")
def handover_files(session_id: str, directory: str) -> list[str]:
    """The files a restarted session is briefed from, newest first.

    Two mechanical sources: the absolute .md paths the session's own transcript names whose
    basename reads like a handover, and the well-known names in the session's working directory
    and its lane scratch directories. Runs only when an alert is about to be sent.
    """
    found: dict[str, float] = {}
    transcript = transcript_of(session_id)
    if transcript:
        path_re = re.compile(r"/[^\s\"'\\]{3,180}?\.md")
        try:
            size = transcript.stat().st_size
            with transcript.open("rb") as handle:
                chunks = [handle.read(2_000_000)]
                if size > 4_000_000:
                    handle.seek(size - 2_000_000)
                    chunks.append(handle.read())
            for chunk in chunks:
                for match in path_re.findall(chunk.decode("utf8", "replace")):
                    if HANDOVER_NAME_RE.search(os.path.basename(match)):
                        try:
                            found[match] = os.path.getmtime(match)
                        except OSError:
                            continue
        except OSError:
            pass
    lane = os.path.basename(directory.rstrip("/")) if directory else ""
    roots = [directory] if directory else []
    if lane:
        roots += [f"/mnt/nvme/sami/{lane}", f"/mnt/nvme/sami/tmp/{lane}"]
    for root in roots:
        for name in HANDOVER_WELL_KNOWN:
            candidate = os.path.join(root, name)
            try:
                found[candidate] = os.path.getmtime(candidate)
            except OSError:
                continue
    return [path for path, _ in sorted(found.items(), key=lambda item: -item[1])][:3]


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the decision


class State(object):
    """The one file this watch owns: the role roster and the open incidents.

    It is opened under an exclusive, non-blocking lock, so a hand run and the timer can never
    both decide that an incident is new -- the loser skips its period. `commit` writes the whole
    file and fsyncs it, and every alert commits before it sends, so the once-per-incident record
    is on disk before the message exists.
    """

    def __init__(self, path: Path) -> None:
        self.path = path
        path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        self.handle = open(path, "a+")
        try:
            fcntl.flock(self.handle, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
            self.locked = True
        except OSError:
            self.locked = False
            self.data = {}
            return
        self.handle.seek(0)
        try:
            self.data = json.loads(self.handle.read() or "{}")
        except ValueError:
            self.data = {}

    def commit(self, options: argparse.Namespace) -> None:
        if options.dry_run and not options.write_state:
            return
        self.handle.seek(0)
        self.handle.truncate()
        json.dump(self.data, self.handle, indent=1, sort_keys=True)
        self.handle.flush()
        os.fsync(self.handle.fileno())

    def close(self) -> None:
        try:
            self.handle.close()
        except OSError:
            pass


def recipients(role: str, record: dict, sessions: list[dict], holder: str) -> tuple[list[tuple[str, str]], list[str], list[str]]:
    """Who hears about `role`: named sessions, the stream lead, the lane's peers, and topics.

    Returns (targets, topics, notes). A target is (session id, why). Envoy keeps no record of
    who recently messaged a role, so the lane's peers are the live holders of the team topics
    the dead session shared -- the sessions whose messages to this role would vanish.
    """
    notes: list[str] = []
    targets = [(named_recipient(CHIEF_OF_STAFF, sessions), "chief of staff"),
               (named_recipient(INFRA_LEAD, sessions), "INFRA lead")]
    directory = record.get("dir") or ""
    leads = [
        session
        for session in sessions
        if session.get("session_id") != holder
        and (session.get("dir") or "") == directory
        and any(LEAD_ROLE_RE.match(name) for name in session.get("roles") or [])
    ]
    leads.sort(key=lambda s: 0 if any(n.startswith("stream-lead-") for n in s.get("roles") or []) else 1)
    if leads:
        lead = leads[0]
        lead_role = next(iter(sorted(lead.get("roles") or [])), "?")
        targets.append((lead["session_id"], f"stream lead ({lead_role})"))
    else:
        notes.append(
            f"No stream lead resolved: no live lead-* or stream-lead-* role holder works in {directory or '(no dir)'}."
        )

    team_topics = [topic for topic in record.get("topics") or [] if topic.startswith(TEAM_TOPIC)]
    team_bases = {topic[:-2] if topic.endswith(".>") else topic for topic in team_topics}
    peers = []
    for session in sessions:
        if session.get("session_id") in {holder} | {t[0] for t in targets}:
            continue
        shared = team_bases & {
            topic[:-2] if topic.endswith(".>") else topic for topic in session.get("topics") or []
        }
        if shared:
            peers.append((session["session_id"], f"lane peer on {sorted(shared)[0]}"))
    if team_bases:
        notes.append(
            "Peers were told on " + ", ".join(sorted(team_bases)) + "."
        )
    else:
        notes.append(
            "Envoy keeps no record of who recently messaged this role, and the unseen session "
            "shared no team topic, so its peers could not be named: only the sessions above were told."
        )
    return targets + peers[:6], sorted(team_bases), notes


def alert_head(role: str, incident: dict, holder: str, now: float, kind: str) -> list[str]:
    """The decision of a role alert, in order: the full text is in the report `compose` writes.

    What a reader must have without opening anything: which role, which holder, how long, what
    is and is not established, and the two steps that cost nothing and cannot hurt a live lane.
    Everything else -- the five sources, the release warning, the handover files, who else was
    told -- is in the report, because a reader who has looked at the pane needs none of it.
    """
    unseen_for = human(now - (incident.get("opened_at") or now))
    return [
        f"ENVOY ROLE HOLDER UNSEEN ({kind}): no source has seen {holder}, the holder of role"
        f" {role}, for {unseen_for}, since {iso(incident.get('opened_at'))}. ESTABLISHED: I"
        " cannot see that session. NOT established: that it stopped.",
        # The body goes on stdin: `envoy send` refuses one given as a command-line word from an
        # agent session, since the shell expands backticks and $(...) in it first. Advice a
        # reader copies has to be advice that runs.
        f"Do: look at its pane, then ask it"
        f" (`envoy send --message-file - {holder} <<<'are you alive?'`). Do NOT"
        " read the role claim or delete the session.",
        f"Lane {incident.get('dir') or '-'} on {incident.get('host') or '-'}.",
    ]


def alert_text(
    role: str, incident: dict, holder: str, now: float, kind: str, banner: list[str] | None = None
) -> str:
    """The alert for one incident. Every value it names was fixed when the incident opened.

    Three rules this text exists to keep. It establishes that the watch cannot see the holder,
    and says so in those words; it never says the holder is dead, which no check here can show.
    It names no call that changes anything: not `omp --resume <id>` in a new pane, which puts a
    second process on a transcript the first may still hold (INFRA-681), and not the role lookup
    or the session delete, each of which acts on a session that may be alive. What it asks for
    instead is what caught the 2026-10-29 false alert in two minutes: look at the pane, and ask
    the session.

    And since 2026-10-30 it says that the holder may not be an omp session at all. Envoy's
    registry takes any registered session id, and the Verified Linux scheduler's envprobe claims
    roles from a process that is not an agent, so "no pane, no transcript, no process, no
    registry entry" is exactly what a non-omp holder looks like, and also what a holder whose
    harness writes its transcript outside the session root looks like. Sending a reader to find
    a pane that never existed and read a transcript that does not exist is how an alert trains
    its reader to skip the next one; steps 1 and 3 now say when they do not apply.
    """
    record = incident
    unseen_for = human(now - (record.get("opened_at") or now))
    transcript = record.get("transcript") or ""
    files = record.get("handover") or []
    handover = (
        "brief it from " + ", ".join(files)
        if files
        else "no handover file found: brief it from the tail of its transcript"
    )
    pane = record.get("pane") or ""
    look = (
        f"Look at pane {pane} -- this watch last saw this session running there."
        if pane
        else "Find its pane: `tmux list-panes -a -F '#{pane_id} #{pane_title} #{pane_current_path}'`"
        f" and look for its title or its directory, {record.get('dir') or '(no dir recorded)'}."
        " THIS STEP MAY NOT APPLY: this watch has never seen this session in a pane, and a"
        " holder that no omp process, no transcript and no registry entry knows may never have"
        " had one."
    )
    swept = record.get("swept") or {}
    if transcript:
        step_three = (
            f"  3. Only once a person has looked and found no live lane: read the tail of"
            f" {transcript}, and restart the lane IN ITS OWN PANE, never a new one -- two omp"
            f" processes on one transcript is the INFRA-681 double loop. {handover}."
        )
    elif swept.get("path") and not swept.get("error"):
        step_three = (
            "  3. Only once a person has looked and found no live lane: its transcript is no"
            " longer on this disk. The session sweep (dispatch://OSSV-240) moved it into Postgres"
            f" omp_sessions at {swept.get('swept_at') or 'an unrecorded time'}, keyed"
            f" {swept['path']}. Restart the lane IN ITS OWN PANE with `omp --resume"
            f" {swept['path']}` -- by that full path: resume by id does not find a session that"
            f" is only in Postgres (dispatch://INFRA-754). {handover}."
        )
    else:
        step_three = (
            "  3. THIS STEP MAY NOT APPLY: no transcript for this holder exists, under"
            f" {SESSION_ROOT} or under its own working directory, so there is nothing to read and"
            " nothing to resume in place. A holder that leaves no transcript here was started by"
            " something other than a person at a pane, and only that thing can start it again."
            + (" Its lane's handover files: " + ", ".join(files) + "." if files else "")
        )
    lines = [
        f"ENVOY ROLE HOLDER UNSEEN ({kind}): nothing this watch can read has seen the holder of"
        f" role {role} for {unseen_for}, since {iso(record.get('opened_at'))}.",
        f"Holder: {holder} \"{record.get('title') or '-'}\" in {record.get('dir') or '-'} on"
        f" {record.get('host') or '-'}.",
        f"ESTABLISHED: {CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER}. NOT established: that the holder has stopped. No check"
        " this watch makes can show that, so treat every line below as a search, not a verdict.",
        "What each source answered when the incident opened:",
    ]
    lines += [f"  - {name}: {sentence}" for name, _, sentence in record.get("sources") or []]
    lines.append(
        "THE HOLDER MAY NOT BE AN omp SESSION AT ALL. Envoy's registry takes any registered"
        " session id (store.Registry.SetRole; registration is an HTTP call), and the Verified"
        " Linux scheduler's envprobe claims roles named node-<deployment>-<number> from a"
        " process that is no agent. Every source above answering \"cannot see\" is as consistent"
        " with a holder this machine's omp-shaped searches can never find -- a non-omp claimer,"
        " or a lane whose harness writes its transcript outside the session root, or a lane"
        " inside an agent box with a uid-shifted /proc -- as it is with a session that stopped."
        " Check the directory line above before you spend a minute on the pane and the"
        " transcript."
    )
    lines.append(
        f"While this holds, a message to notifications.role.{role} may reach nobody, and the"
        " claim may still be registered to this holder. This watch does not read the claim, and"
        " nor should you while the lane may be alive: GET /v1/roles/<role> releases a lapsed"
        " holder's claim as it answers (checked against the deployed listener on"
        " 2026-10-29). Steps 1 and 2 below settle the same question without touching anything."
    )
    lines += [
        "Do, in this order, and stop as soon as one answers:",
        f"  1. {look}",
        "  2. Ask the session. Type a question into that pane, or `envoy send"
        f" --message-file - {holder} <<<'are you alive?'`. A session that answers is alive and this alert is wrong:"
        " say so on the incident and nothing else needs doing. That is how the 2026-10-29"
        " 09:20:30Z false alert on bet64-eta-arity was caught, two minutes in.",
        step_three,
        f"  4. {RELEASE_CALL.format(role=role, holder=holder)}",
    ]
    lines += record.get("notes") or []
    lines += banner or []
    lines.append(
        "From envoy-role-holder-watch (~/.dotfiles/scripts/envoy-role-holder-watch) on "
        f"{socket.gethostname()}, a systemd --user timer every 30 s."
    )
    return guard_verdict("\n".join(lines))


def clear_text(
    role: str, incident: dict, holder: str, cause: str, now: float, kind: str = "seen",
    banner: list[str] | None = None,
) -> str:
    """The one message that ends an incident. Two kinds, and they claim different things.

    `seen`: a source found the holder, so the role has a holder this watch can see again.

    `quiet`: NOTHING found the holder and nothing ever will, and the only reason to stop is the
    fifth source -- a named file in the holder's working directory, written at a named time.
    That text must not say the lane is working, because an mtime cannot say it: it says which
    file was written and when, says what it does not establish, and says that the watch will
    not repeat this alert. See DEFAULT_DIR_GRACE for why that is the right trade.
    """
    opened = incident.get("opened_at") or now
    tail = (
        f" The unseen holder was {holder}; the incident opened at {iso(opened)}, cleared at"
        f" {iso(now)}, and lasted {human(now - opened)}."
    )
    if kind == "elsewhere":
        return guard_verdict(
            f"ENVOY ROLE HOLDER OUTSIDE THIS HOST: closed this host's alert on {role}. {cause}."
            " A remote registration does not establish that the holder is alive." + tail
        )
    if kind == "quiet":
        lines = [
            f"ENVOY ROLE HOLDER UNSEEN, NOT ALERTING AGAIN: role {role}'s holder is still unseen"
            " and this watch has no source that will ever see it, so it stops here rather than"
            " repeat an alert nobody can answer.",
            f"The only thing that moved: {cause}.",
            "WHAT THAT DOES NOT SAY: it does not say the lane is working, and it does not say"
            " the holder is alive. A file's mtime says that file was written. On 2026-10-30"
            " ps.log in such a directory grew about a kilobyte a second while the probe's own"
            " outputs had stopped three minutes earlier, so judge the named file yourself:"
            " work, or a log beside it.",
            "WHAT IT DOES SAY: something in that lane's directory has been written inside the"
            " last few minutes, while no omp process, no transcript, no Envoy registry entry"
            " and no open file descriptor on this machine can name the holder. That is the"
            " shape of a holder this watch cannot see by construction -- a claimer that is not"
            " an omp session, or a lane whose transcript lives outside the session root -- not"
            " of a lane that died with nobody noticing.",
            f"{role} is left in the watch's state file as quieted, so no further alert and no"
            " escalation goes out for this holder. From here this watch reads only the two free"
            " sources for it -- an omp process, and Envoy's own entry -- so if either sees the"
            " holder again the role goes back to being watched normally from that moment. The"
            " walk of its directory and of the session root is not repeated, because nothing"
            " they could find would change what this message says."
            + tail,
        ]
    elif kind == "suppressed":
        lines = [
            f"ENVOY ROLE HOLDER UNSEEN, CLOSED BY A SUPPRESSION: the open alert on role {role}"
            " is closed because its whole class is now suppressed, not because anything was"
            " learnt about the holder. It is still unseen.",
            f"The suppression: {cause}. It is named, dated and counted in every alert of every"
            " other class this watch sends, and the alerts come back by themselves when it"
            " expires." + tail,
        ]
    elif kind == "exited":
        lines = [
            f"ENVOY ROLE HOLDER EXITED NORMALLY: role {role}'s {cause}. The last entry of its"
            " transcript is a normal session_exit, so the session ended on purpose and this"
            " watch will not alert on this holder again. The role still names it; whoever"
            " needs the role must claim it." + tail,
        ]
    else:
        lines = [
            f"ENVOY ROLE HOLDER UNSEEN, CLEARED: role {role} has a holder this watch can see"
            f" again. What cleared it: {cause}." + tail + " No action is needed.",
        ]
    lines += banner or []
    lines.append("From envoy-role-holder-watch on " + socket.gethostname() + ".")
    return guard_verdict("\n".join(lines))


def clear_head(role: str, incident: dict, holder: str, cause: str, now: float, kind: str) -> list[str]:
    """The decision of a clear, in order of what a reader needs: the full text is in the report."""
    opened = incident.get("opened_at") or now
    lasted = f"The incident opened {iso(opened)} and lasted {human(now - opened)}."
    if kind == "elsewhere":
        return [
            f"ENVOY ROLE HOLDER OUTSIDE THIS HOST: closed this host's alert on {role}. {cause}.",
            "A remote registration does not establish that the holder is alive.",
            lasted,
        ]
    if kind == "quiet":
        return [
            f"ENVOY ROLE HOLDER UNSEEN, NOT ALERTING AGAIN: role {role}'s holder {holder} is still"
            " unseen and no source here will ever see it, so this watch stops rather than repeat"
            " an alert nobody can answer.",
            f"The only thing that moved: {cause[:200]}.",
            "That does not say the lane is working and does not say the holder is alive.",
            lasted,
        ]
    if kind == "suppressed":
        return [
            f"ENVOY ROLE HOLDER UNSEEN, CLOSED BY A SUPPRESSION: the open alert on role {role} is"
            f" closed because its class is suppressed, not because anything was learnt about"
            f" holder {holder}. It is still unseen.",
            lasted,
        ]
    if kind == "exited":
        return [
            f"ENVOY ROLE HOLDER EXITED NORMALLY: role {role}'s {cause}",
            "The session ended on purpose, so this watch will not alert on this holder again."
            " Whoever needs the role must claim it.",
            lasted,
        ]
    return [
        f"ENVOY ROLE HOLDER UNSEEN, CLEARED: role {role} has a holder this watch can see again."
        " No action is needed.",
        f"What cleared it: {cause[:200]}.",
        lasted,
    ]


def send_clear(
    envoy: Envoy, options: argparse.Namespace, role: str, incident: dict, holder: str, cause: str,
    now: float, kind: str = "seen", banner: list[str] | None = None,
) -> bool:
    """Tell everyone who heard the alert how the incident ended. One per incident."""
    if not incident.get("alerted_at") or incident.get("cleared_at"):
        return False
    incident["cleared_at"] = now
    targets = incident.get("told") or [CHIEF_OF_STAFF, INFRA_LEAD]
    text, report = compose(
        options, f"role-{role}-{kind}", clear_text(role, incident, holder, cause, now, kind, banner),
        now, *clear_head(role, incident, holder, cause, now, kind),
    )
    told = [target for target in targets if options.dry_run or envoy.send(target, text)]
    named = {
        "quiet": "QUIETED ON THE DIRECTORY RULE", "suppressed": "CLOSED BY A SUPPRESSION",
        "exited": "CLOSED, HOLDER EXITED NORMALLY",
    }
    log(f"{role}: {named.get(kind, 'CLEARED')} ({cause});"
        f" {'would tell' if options.dry_run else 'told'} {len(told)}/{len(targets)} sessions;"
        f" full report {report}")
    if options.print_alert:
        print(text)
    return True

# ------------------------------------------------------------- agent panes left at a shell
#
# Why: after the 2026-10-27 13:56Z reboot, five of six Verified Linux panes came back as a bash
# prompt. Their omp never started, so no process vanished, and only one held an Envoy role, so the
# role check above saw one of six. This check reads the panes themselves.

SHELLS = {"bash", "sh", "zsh", "fish", "dash"}
RESURRECT_DIR = Path.home() / ".local/share/tmux/resurrect"
PANE_FIELDS = (
    "pid", "start_time", "pane_id", "session_name", "window_index", "window_name",
    "pane_index", "pane_current_command", "pane_pid", "pane_current_path", "pane_title",
    # A pane that runs a ONE-SHOT omp and is meant to outlive it sets this pane option:
    #   tmux set -p @agent_oneshot 1
    # Why it exists: the grading lane's review.sh opens a tmux session per gate, runs a one-shot
    # `omp -p` reviewer that exits after it posts its verdict, then runs the merge script and a
    # `sleep` to keep the pane. That pane is at a shell with no omp in it BY DESIGN, and on
    # 2026-10-30 between 03:46Z and 03:53Z it produced three alerts in seven minutes for three
    # healthy gates. The watch cannot tell that pane from a lane whose omp died -- both are a
    # shell with an agent's title -- so the pane says so itself, and the lane that opens it is
    # the only party that knows.
    "@agent_oneshot",
)


@bounded_step("tmux list-panes")
def tmux_panes() -> list[dict] | None:
    """Every pane of the default tmux server, once each (grouped sessions list a pane per group
    member). None when tmux could not be read; [] when no server runs."""
    fmt = "\t".join("#{" + field + "}" for field in PANE_FIELDS)
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(
            ["tmux", "list-panes", "-a", "-F", fmt],
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as error:
        log(f"panes: tmux list-panes failed: {error}")
        return None
    if done.returncode != 0:
        if "no server running" in done.stderr or "error connecting" in done.stderr:
            # Said out loud: a watch that silently sees no pane is the failure this check exists
            # to catch. The socket is $TMUX_TMPDIR/tmux-<uid>/default, which .bashrc sets and the
            # service unit repeats, since a systemd service sources no shell.
            log(f"panes: {done.stderr.strip()} (TMUX_TMPDIR={os.environ.get('TMUX_TMPDIR', '(unset)')})")
            return []
        log(f"panes: tmux list-panes exited {done.returncode}: {done.stderr.strip()}")
        return None
    panes: dict[str, dict] = {}
    for line in done.stdout.splitlines():
        values = line.split("\t", len(PANE_FIELDS) - 1)
        if len(values) == len(PANE_FIELDS):
            pane = dict(zip(PANE_FIELDS, values))
            panes.setdefault(pane["pane_id"], pane)
    return list(panes.values())


def process_table() -> tuple[dict[int, list[int]], dict[int, tuple[str, int]]]:
    """children by parent pid, and (comm, foreground process group of its tty) by pid.

    The run's own process scan (`omp_sessions_by_pid`) already read /proc/<pid>/stat of every
    process this user can see, so this returns what that pass built. It walks /proc itself only
    when no scan has run in this process -- a test that stubs the scan, or a caller that asks
    for the table first. One pass of 3,400 processes was 0.2-0.5 s of CPU and up to 9 s of wall
    clock at load 268 (2026-11-03 02:41Z), so two of them were a tenth of a slow run.
    """
    if _PROC_TABLE is not None:
        return _PROC_TABLE
    return read_process_table()


@bounded_step("the process table", whole_budget=True)
def read_process_table() -> tuple[dict[int, list[int]], dict[int, tuple[str, int]]]:
    """`process_table` when no scan has built it: one pass over /proc/<pid>/stat."""
    children: dict[int, list[int]] = {}
    info: dict[int, tuple[str, int]] = {}
    for entry in os.listdir(PROC_ROOT):
        if not entry.isdigit():
            continue
        check_time("the process table")
        stat = read_stat(entry)
        if stat is None:
            continue
        comm, ppid, tpgid = stat
        children.setdefault(ppid, []).append(pid := int(entry))
        info[pid] = (comm, tpgid)
    return children, info


def title_text(title: str) -> str:
    """An omp pane title without its `π` mark and spinner frame: `π ⠼ Fix X` -> `Fix X`."""
    parts = title.split(" ", 2)
    return parts[2].strip() if len(parts) == 3 and parts[0] == "π" else title.strip()


def session_dir_of(cwd: str) -> Path:
    """omp's transcript directory for a working directory (-src-x under $HOME, --abs-path-- else)."""
    home = str(Path.home())
    if cwd == home or cwd.startswith(home + "/"):
        return SESSION_ROOT / ("-" + cwd[len(home) + 1 :].replace("/", "-"))
    return SESSION_ROOT / ("--" + cwd.strip("/").replace("/", "-") + "--")


@bounded_step("the pane directory's transcript titles")
def transcript_by_title(cwd: str, text: str, live: dict[str, int]) -> tuple[str, Path] | None:
    """The newest top-level transcript in the pane's directory whose title (its first line) the
    pane title carries, and whose session no live process holds."""
    best: tuple[float, str, Path] | None = None
    try:
        candidates = list(session_dir_of(cwd).glob("*.jsonl"))
    except OSError:
        return None
    for candidate in candidates:
        try:
            with candidate.open("rb") as handle:
                first = json.loads(handle.readline(4096) or b"{}")
            stamp = candidate.stat().st_mtime
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            continue
        title = (first.get("title") or "").strip() if isinstance(first, dict) else ""
        ids = UUID_RE.findall(candidate.name)
        if not title or not ids or title not in text or ids[-1] in live:
            continue
        if best is None or stamp > best[0]:
            best = (stamp, ids[-1], candidate)
    return (best[1], best[2]) if best else None


@bounded_step("the pane directory's inventory")
def directory_inventory(cwd: str, live: dict[str, int], limit: int = 3) -> str:
    """One line saying what the pane's session directory DOES hold, for an unresolved pane.

    "No transcript carries the pane's title" reads as "there is nothing there", and on
    2026-11-01 pane %450 (/home/sami/src/eng-ops) got that line while the directory held one
    transcript, of a LIVE session: the association was wrong, not missing, and a reader had to
    look the directory up to learn which. So the alert names the count and, for up to `limit`
    newest, the session id, its title, and whether an omp process holds it now.
    """
    directory = session_dir_of(cwd)
    entries: list[tuple[float, str, str]] = []
    try:
        candidates = list(directory.glob("*.jsonl"))
    except OSError:
        candidates = []
    for candidate in candidates:
        ids = UUID_RE.findall(candidate.name)
        try:
            stamp = candidate.stat().st_mtime
            with candidate.open("rb") as handle:
                first = json.loads(handle.readline(4096) or b"{}")
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            continue
        title = (first.get("title") or "").strip() if isinstance(first, dict) else ""
        entries.append((stamp, ids[-1] if ids else candidate.name, title))
    if not entries:
        return (
            f"That directory holds no transcript at all ({directory}), so no omp session has"
            " ever written one for this working directory on this machine."
        )
    entries.sort(reverse=True)
    shown = entries[:limit]
    described = []
    for _, session_id, title in shown:
        pid = live.get(session_id)
        described.append(
            f"{session_id} ({json.dumps(title) if title else 'no title'}),"
            + (f" live in pid {pid}" if pid else " not running")
        )
    word = "transcript" if len(entries) == 1 else "transcripts"
    head = f"That directory holds {len(entries)} {word}"
    head += f", of which the {len(shown)} newest are: " if len(entries) > len(shown) else ": "
    return (
        head
        + "; ".join(described)
        + ". A live one is not this pane's; one not running did not carry the pane's title."
    )


@bounded_step("the tmux-resurrect saves")
def resurrect_command(pane: dict, text: str, limit: int = 60) -> tuple[str, str] | None:
    """The newest tmux-resurrect save in which this pane's place (session, window, pane index),
    under the same title, ran `omp --resume`: (its command, the save file)."""
    try:
        saves = sorted(RESURRECT_DIR.glob("tmux_resurrect_*.txt"), reverse=True)[:limit]
    except OSError:
        return None
    place = (pane["session_name"], pane["window_index"], pane["pane_index"])
    for save in saves:
        try:
            lines = save.read_text(errors="replace").splitlines()
        except OSError:
            continue
        for line in lines:
            fields = line.split("\t")
            if len(fields) < 11 or fields[0] != "pane" or (fields[1], fields[2], fields[5]) != place:
                continue
            command = fields[10].lstrip(":")
            if "--resume" in command and title_text(fields[6]) == text:
                words = command.split()
                if words and words[0].endswith("/omp"):
                    words[0] = "omp"
                return " ".join(words), str(save)
    return None


@bounded_step("the resumed transcript's header")
def resume_target(command: str) -> tuple[str | None, str | None]:
    """The session and transcript an `omp … --resume <value>` command actually resumes.

    `--resume` takes an id OR a path, and a path is where the 2026-10-29 blind spot lives a
    second time: `omp --resume .../<stamp>_<parent id>/ArityLane.jsonl` carries the PARENT's
    uuid in its directory name and none of its own, so taking the last uuid anywhere in the
    command names a different, possibly live session. The pane alert then checks the wrong
    transcript for holders, finds none, and tells a reader to start a second omp on a lane that
    is running. Read the path's header instead; fall back to a bare uuid argument.
    """
    words = command.split()
    value = ""
    for index, word in enumerate(words):
        if word in ("--resume", "--restore") and index + 1 < len(words):
            value = words[index + 1]
            break
        if word.startswith(("--resume=", "--restore=")):
            value = word.split("=", 1)[1]
            break
    value = value.strip("'\"")
    if not value:
        return None, None
    if UUID_RE.fullmatch(value):
        return value, None
    # Anything else is a path. Its header is the ONLY thing that names its session: the uuids
    # in the path itself are its parent's and its grandparent's, and naming one of those is how
    # the pane alert ends up checking a live lane's sibling for holders. A header this watch
    # cannot read leaves the session unresolved, which the alert says out loud, rather than
    # guessed from the path.
    return session_id_in_header(value), value



def move_alert_text(incident: dict, now: float) -> str:
    """A mover pane that has not finished. It never says how to restart anything.

    `ossv-agent move` stops the worker on this box, pushes its tree and resumes it on another,
    so a `[Stopped by SIGTERM]` and a shell prompt here are the move working. The only thing
    worth saying is that the move is taking long, and where the worker is now.
    """
    move = incident["move"]
    where_now = move.get("where") or (
        f"Where {move['worker']} runs now is not recorded yet: no MOVED.md under"
        f" {LANE_WORK_ROOT / 'workers' / move['worker']} and no row in {COLD_WORKERS_TSV}."
    )
    return guard_verdict(
        "\n".join(
            [
                f"WORKER MOVE NOT FINISHED: tmux pane {incident['pane_id']} ({incident['where']},"
                f" window \"{incident['window_name']}\") is the mover session {move['mover']}, which"
                f" relocates worker {move['worker']} off {socket.gethostname()}. It has sat at a"
                f" {incident['shell']} prompt for {human(now - incident['opened_at'])}, since"
                f" {iso(incident['opened_at'])}.",
                "This is NOT a dead lane and nothing here should be resumed: a move stops the"
                " worker on this box (its pane shows `[Stopped by SIGTERM]` and the mover's"
                " recovery lines), pushes its tree, and resumes the same session id on the other"
                " box. Starting omp in this pane would put a second process on that session.",
                f"Where the worker is: {where_now}",
                "Do: look at the mover pane and at `ossv-agent move` on the box that runs it."
                " Only the mover knows whether it is still pushing or has stopped.",
                f"From envoy-role-holder-watch on {socket.gethostname()}. One alert per mover"
                " pane; it clears when the pane goes away or omp runs in it again.",
            ]
        )
    )


def pane_alert_text(incident: dict, now: float) -> str:
    where = incident["where"]
    session = incident.get("session")
    lines = [
        f"AGENT PANE AT SHELL: tmux pane {incident['pane_id']} ({where}, window"
        f" \"{incident['window_name']}\") has sat at a {incident['shell']} prompt with no omp in it for"
        f" {human(now - incident['opened_at'])}, first seen at {iso(incident['opened_at'])}.",
        f"Evidence: the pane's foreground process group is its own shell (pid {incident['shell_pid']},"
        f" /proc/<pid>/stat tpgid), no omp process descends from it, and its title still reads"
        f" \"{incident['title']}\" (omp sets that `π` title; a plain shell never does). cwd {incident['cwd']}.",
    ]
    last_write = incident.get("last_write")
    # The one fact that separates a planned wind-down from a crash gets a line of its own: OSS
    # Verify read 00:51:59Z off the first of these alerts and recognised its own planned stop.
    lines.append(
        f"LAST TRANSCRIPT WRITE: {iso(last_write)}"
        + (f" ({human(now - last_write)} before this alert)." if last_write
           else " -- no transcript resolved, so there is no last write to compare.")
        + " A write at the moment the lane was wound down reads as a planned stop. One that"
        " stops mid-work does NOT establish that it died -- a session can stop writing while it"
        " waits on a model or a long tool call, and this watch cannot tell the two apart."
    )
    if session:
        lines.append(f"Last session: {session} ({incident['session_source']}).")
        if incident.get("transcript"):
            lines.append(f"Transcript: {incident['transcript']}.")
    else:
        lines.append(
            "Last session: NOT RESOLVED -- this watch never saw omp run here, no transcript in"
            f" {session_dir_of(incident['cwd'])} carries the pane's title, and no tmux-resurrect save"
            " records a resume command for this place. Read the newest transcripts there."
        )
        lines.append(incident.get("directory_inventory") or directory_inventory(incident["cwd"], {}))
    files = incident.get("handover") or []
    brief = "brief it from " + ", ".join(files) if files else "no handover file found: brief it from the tail of its transcript"
    lines.append(
        "ESTABLISHED: this pane is at a shell. NOT established: that its session has stopped --"
        " the session may be running in another pane, in a box, or under another process."
    )
    # The recheck has to be runnable BY THE READER. Every lead on this box reads its Envoy
    # messages from inside an agent box, and a box sees neither host pids nor host /proc -- so
    # the `ps` and `ls -l /proc/*/fd` below answer "nothing there" from a box whatever the host
    # is doing. On 2026-11-05 at 21:21:40Z this alert fired correctly for pane %37 (its omp had
    # been OOM-killed at 21:17:57Z); the lane's own restart began about 100 s later; the lead ran
    # pgrep from inside their box, got the only answer it could give, and typed into a live
    # restart. `omp-session-live` is the fleet's liveness test precisely because it works from
    # either side -- it asks the host scan and `docker exec`s into boxes.
    live = (f"`omp-session-live {session}`, which works from inside an agent box"
            if session else "there is no session id to check, so find the lane before starting one")
    lines.append(
        f"THIS DESCRIBES {iso(now)}, NOT THE MOMENT YOU READ IT. A pane can be restarted between the"
        " alert and its delivery, so the first step is always to re-read the pane, never to send keys:"
        f" {live}, then `tmux capture-pane -p -t {incident['pane_id']} | tail -n 15`."
        " Either an omp line in the pane or a live answer means the lane is already running and"
        " nothing is owed."
        f" FROM THE HOST ONLY, a third reading: `ps -eo pid,ppid,stat,etime,args --no-headers |"
        f" awk '$2=={incident['shell_pid']}'` -- a foreground child there is a restart in progress."
        " That one reads host pids and answers nothing from inside a box; do not take its silence"
        " for an idle pane."
    )
    if incident.get("restart"):
        target = incident.get("transcript") or ""
        check = (
            f"check first that no process holds its transcript"
            f" (`ls -l /proc/*/fd 2>/dev/null | grep -F {shlex.quote(target)}`, HOST ONLY)"
            if target
            else "this watch resolved no transcript for it, so there is nothing to check for"
            " holders: find the lane before you start a second one"
        )
        lines.append(
            f"Do, only if the pane is still at a shell: {check}, and only then restart it IN THAT PANE,"
            f" never a new one:"
            f" `tmux send-keys -t {incident['pane_id']} {shlex.quote(incident['restart'])} Enter`."
            f" Then {brief}."
        )
        lines.append(
            "Automatic restart: NOT RUN. This watch never restarts a pane: a paused pane and a dead"
            " one look the same to a script, so look at the pane and decide."
        )
    else:
        lines.append(f"Do: start the lane again in that pane, never a new one, and {brief}.")
    lines += incident.get("notes") or []
    told = incident.get("told_why") or []
    if told:
        lines.append("Told: " + "; ".join(told) + ".")
    lines.append(
        "One alert per pane per incident, one clear when omp runs there again. From"
        f" envoy-role-holder-watch (~/.dotfiles/scripts/envoy-role-holder-watch) on {socket.gethostname()}."
    )
    return guard_verdict("\n".join(lines))


def pane_head(incident: dict, now: float) -> list[str]:
    """The decision of a pane alert, in order: the full text is in the report.

    The first 200 characters carry what, where and how long, because that is all a reader saw
    of the 2,404-character alert of 2026-11-01T10:01Z before deciding whether to read on.
    """
    if incident.get("move"):
        move = incident["move"]
        where_now = (move.get("where") or "")[:160]
        return [
            f"WORKER MOVE NOT FINISHED: tmux pane {incident['pane_id']} ({incident['where']}) is"
            f" mover session {move['mover']}, moving worker {move['worker']} off"
            f" {socket.gethostname()}. It has sat at a {incident['shell']} prompt for"
            f" {human(now - incident['opened_at'])}.",
            "This is NOT a dead lane: resume nothing here, and start no omp in that pane.",
            f"Where the worker is: {where_now}" if where_now
            else "Where the worker runs now is not recorded yet (see the report).",
            "Do: look at the mover pane and at `ossv-agent move` on the box that runs it.",
        ]
    session = incident.get("session")
    return [
        f"AGENT PANE AT SHELL: tmux pane {incident['pane_id']} ({incident['where']}, window"
        f" \"{incident['window_name']}\") has had no omp in it for"
        f" {human(now - incident['opened_at'])}, since {iso(incident['opened_at'])}.",
        f"Do: re-read the pane (`tmux capture-pane -p -t {incident['pane_id']} | tail -n 15`)"
        f" AND ask the host whether the session still runs"
        f" (`ssh {socket.gethostname()} omp-session-live {session}`)."
        " Restart the lane IN THAT PANE, never a new one, only if BOTH say so: the pane is still"
        " a shell and no process holds the session. A pane can be a shell while its session runs"
        " elsewhere, and on 2026-11-07 that nearly produced a twin."
        " Do not use pgrep from inside a box: it sees only that box's processes and answers 0 for"
        " a session running on the host. This watch restarted nothing."
        if session else
        f"Do: re-read the pane (`tmux capture-pane -p -t {incident['pane_id']} | tail -n 15`)."
        " The session is NOT RESOLVED, so there is nothing to test for liveness and nothing to"
        " resume; find the session first. This watch restarted nothing.",
        f"Last session: {session}." if session else "Last session: NOT RESOLVED.",
        f"cwd {incident['cwd']}.",
    ]


# The lane boxes' operator. A worker pane under /home/sami/work/workers/** on an ossv-agent-* box
# has no lead-* holder in its own directory, so every one of its alerts fell through to the INFRA
# lead, who holds neither that pane's tmux socket nor the lane. The operator holds both. Measured
# 2026-10-30: five such alerts in thirteen minutes, all from one sshfs restart pass, none of them
# actionable by the recipient. The operator asked for them in writing at 08:55:41Z.
LANE_WORKER_DIR_RE = re.compile(r"^/home/sami/work/(?:cold/)?workers/")
# ossv-worker's own records on a lane box, home-relative so nothing here hard-codes a user:
#   <home>/work/.ossv-workers/<lane>/lead     the session id that leads that worker
#   <home>/work/.ossv-workers/<lane>/session  the worker's own live session id
#   <home>/work/workers/<lane>/MOVED.md       written when a move finishes, naming the new box
#   <home>/work/cold/workers.tsv              one row per worker; `vm` is the box it runs on
LANE_WORK_ROOT = Path.home() / "work"
OSSV_WORKER_STATE = LANE_WORK_ROOT / ".ossv-workers"
COLD_WORKERS_TSV = LANE_WORK_ROOT / "cold/workers.tsv"
LANE_NAME_RE = re.compile(r"/workers/(?P<lane>[^/]+)")
# `ossv-agent move` runs each relocation in a tmux session of its own, named for the worker it
# is moving. Its pane shows the worker's `[Stopped by SIGTERM]` and the mover's recovery lines,
# which is exactly the shape of a lane that died -- and is not one.
MOVER_SESSION_RE = re.compile(r"^mv-(?P<worker>.+)$")


def lane_of(directory: str) -> str:
    """The ossv-worker lane name a pane's working directory belongs to, or ""."""
    found = LANE_NAME_RE.search(directory or "")
    return found.group("lane") if found else ""


def worker_record(lane: str, name: str) -> str:
    """One file of ossv-worker's state for a lane (`lead`, `session`), stripped, or ""."""
    if not lane:
        return ""
    try:
        return (OSSV_WORKER_STATE / lane / name).read_text().strip()
    except OSError:
        return ""


def workers_tsv_row(worker: str) -> dict[str, str]:
    """The `<home>/work/cold/workers.tsv` row for a worker, keyed by its header, or {}."""
    try:
        lines = COLD_WORKERS_TSV.read_text().splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return {}
    if not lines:
        return {}
    header = lines[0].split("\t")
    for line in lines[1:]:
        fields = line.split("\t")
        if fields and fields[0] == worker:
            return {key: value for key, value in zip(header, fields)}
    return {}


def moved_to(worker: str) -> str:
    """Where a worker runs now, from ossv-worker's own records, or "".

    MOVED.md first: the mover writes it when the move finishes, and it names the new box and
    says the session id is unchanged. Failing that, the `vm` column of the workers table.
    """
    try:
        first = (LANE_WORK_ROOT / "workers" / worker / "MOVED.md").read_text().splitlines()[0].strip()
    except (OSError, IndexError):
        first = ""
    if first:
        return first
    row = workers_tsv_row(worker)
    if row.get("vm"):
        return f"{worker} is recorded on {row['vm']} in {COLD_WORKERS_TSV}."
    return ""


@bounded_step("the move records")
def move_in_progress(pane: dict) -> dict | None:
    """What a pane says about a move of its own worker, or None when it is not a mover pane."""
    found = MOVER_SESSION_RE.match(pane.get("session_name") or "")
    if not found:
        return None
    worker = found.group("worker")
    return {"mover": pane["session_name"], "worker": worker, "where": moved_to(worker)}


# `agentbox move --to <machine>` keeps MOVE_TO_ACTIVE/<session id> (`pid=<relauncher>`,
# `target=`, `start=`) while it moves a session to another machine, and leaves the marker
# `agentbox-moved` (`from=`, `to=`, `time=`) in the session's directory and as MOVED_INDEX/<id>
# once it has. A stale active file whose relauncher died holds nothing, and a marker counts
# only on the machine its `from=` names. Pane %1740 alerted at 05:12:57Z and %1741 at 05:35:37Z
# on 2026-11-03 while
# such moves of their sessions ran.
MOVE_TO_ACTIVE = Path.home() / ".cache" / "omp" / "agentbox" / "move-to" / "active"
MOVED_MARKER = "agentbox-moved"
MOVED_INDEX = Path.home() / ".omp" / "agentbox-moved"


def key_values(path: Path) -> dict[str, str]:
    """A file of `key=value` lines as a dict, or {} when it cannot be read."""
    try:
        text = path.read_text(errors="replace")
    except OSError:
        return {}
    return dict(line.split("=", 1) for line in text.splitlines() if "=" in line)


def move_claim(session: str, transcript: str = "") -> str:
    """Why an `agentbox move` owns `session` now, or "": a move --to running here, or a marker
    saying the session moved away from this machine."""
    if not UUID_RE.fullmatch(session or ""):
        return ""
    fields = key_values(MOVE_TO_ACTIVE / session)
    pid = fields.get("pid", "")
    if pid.isdigit() and (PROC_ROOT / pid).exists():
        return (
            f"agentbox move --to {fields.get('target') or 'another machine'} owns session {session}"
            f" (relauncher pid {pid})"
        )
    here = os.environ.get("AGENTBOX_HOST") or socket.gethostname().split(".")[0]
    markers = [MOVED_INDEX / session]
    if transcript:
        markers.append(Path(transcript).with_suffix("") / MOVED_MARKER)
    for marker in markers:
        fields = key_values(marker)
        if fields and fields.get("from") == here:
            return (
                f"session {session} moved to {fields.get('to') or 'another machine'} at"
                f" {fields.get('time') or 'an unknown time'} and runs there now (marker {marker})"
            )
    return ""


# agentbox-run's own end line, verbatim from two panes on 2026-11-06:
#   ... agentbox-c63e8d4dca13: omp (pid 1621) exited after 20732s (exit code 0, signal none); the box ends
#   ... agentbox-57dd6193d84d: omp (pid 2184) exited after 67631s (exit code 137, signal SIGKILL); the box ends
# Both halves are read: a lane killed by earlyoom reports 137/SIGKILL and must still alert.
BOX_EXIT_RE = re.compile(
    r"omp \(pid \d+\) exited after \S+ \(exit code (-?\d+), signal (\S+?)\); the box ends"
)


@bounded_step("the pane's box-exit line")
def box_exit_code(pane_id: str) -> tuple[int, str] | None:
    """(exit code, signal) agentbox printed for this pane's omp, or None if it printed none.

    The LAST such line only: a pane that ran several boxed sessions holds one per session, and
    the one that matters is the end of the most recent. A capture that fails, or a pane whose
    scrollback no longer holds the line, returns None and the pane is judged by everything else.

    -J joins wrapped lines. Without it tmux returns the line in terminal-width pieces and this
    never matches: the first attempt at this check read `omp (pid 1621) exited` and `exit code 0`
    as two lines and found nothing.
    """
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(
            ["tmux", "capture-pane", "-p", "-J", "-t", pane_id, "-S", "-200"],
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10, check=False,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    if out.returncode != 0:
        return None
    found = BOX_EXIT_RE.findall(out.stdout)
    if not found:
        return None
    code, signal = found[-1]
    return int(code), signal


@bounded_step("the pane's release check")
def pane_release(record: dict, session: str | None, transcript: str) -> str:
    """Why a pane whose omp is gone is NOT at a shell in the sense this check alerts on, or "".

    A pane counts only when its last omp died without meaning to. Three things say it meant to:
    the omp was a one-shot (`omp -p`, which processes its prompt and exits); an `agentbox move`
    owns its session; or its session's transcript ends in a normal session_exit. On 2026-11-03
    review panes %2177 and %2176 alerted three minutes after their sessions exited normally, and
    %1740 and %1741 while their sessions were being moved to sami-devbox. What is left -- an omp
    that died with no exit record and no move -- is what this check is for.
    """
    if record.get("oneshot"):
        return "its omp ran a one-shot prompt (`omp -p`), which exits by design"
    for candidate in [session] + [s for s in record.get("sessions") or [] if s != session]:
        claim = move_claim(candidate or "", transcript if candidate == session else "")
        if claim:
            return claim
    if session and UUID_RE.fullmatch(session):
        path = transcript or str(transcript_of(session, walk=False) or "")
        ended = normal_exit_at(path) if path else None
        if ended:
            return f"its session {session} ended with a normal session_exit at {ended}"
    return ""


@bounded_step("the lane's owner records")
def pane_recipients(directory: str, session: str | None, sessions: list[dict]) -> tuple[list[tuple[str, str]], list[str]]:
    infra_lead = named_recipient(INFRA_LEAD, sessions)
    operator = named_recipient(LANE_BOX_OPERATOR, sessions)
    targets = [(infra_lead, "INFRA lead")]
    live = {s.get("session_id") for s in sessions}
    leads = [
        s for s in sessions
        if s.get("session_id") not in {session, infra_lead}
        and (s.get("dir") or "") == directory
        and any(LEAD_ROLE_RE.match(name) for name in s.get("roles") or [])
    ]
    leads.sort(key=lambda s: 0 if any(n.startswith("stream-lead-") for n in s.get("roles") or []) else 1)
    if leads:
        role = next(iter(sorted(leads[0].get("roles") or [])), "?")
        targets.append((leads[0]["session_id"], f"stream lead ({role})"))
        return targets, []
    notes = [f"No live lead-* or stream-lead-* role holder works in {directory or '(no dir)'}."]
    # No role holder in the pane's own directory. A lane worker still has a named owner, and it
    # is not a role: ossv-worker records it in <home>/work/.ossv-workers/<lane>/lead. Verified
    # live against Envoy's session list before it is used, because an id in a file is a dated
    # claim: the lead it names can itself have been restarted under a new id.
    lane = lane_of(directory or "")
    recorded = worker_record(lane, "lead")
    if recorded and recorded not in {session, infra_lead} and UUID_RE.fullmatch(recorded):
        if recorded in live:
            targets.append((recorded, f"lane lead of {lane}, from ossv-worker's own record"))
            notes.append(
                f"The lane's owner came from ossv-worker's record {OSSV_WORKER_STATE / lane / 'lead'},"
                " not from a role, and is live on Envoy."
            )
        else:
            notes.append(
                f"ossv-worker records {recorded} as the lead of {lane}"
                f" ({OSSV_WORKER_STATE / lane / 'lead'}), but that session is not live, so it was"
                " not told."
            )
    if LANE_WORKER_DIR_RE.match(directory or "") and operator != session:
        # The operator of the box holds the tmux socket the restart command needs.
        if operator in live:
            targets.append((operator, "lane-box operator"))
            notes.append(
                "The lane-box operator was told too: it holds the tmux socket the restart"
                " command needs."
            )
        else:
            notes.append(f"The lane-box operator ({operator}) is not live.")
    return targets, notes


def check_panes(options: argparse.Namespace, state: State, envoy: Envoy, sessions: list[dict], held: dict[str, int], now: float) -> tuple[int, int, int, int]:
    """(agent panes, agent panes at a shell, alerts, clears) for the default tmux server."""
    try:
        panes = tmux_panes()
    except StepCut as cut:
        log(f"panes: not checked this run: {cut}")
        return 0, 0, 0, 0
    if panes is None:
        return 0, 0, 0, 0
    memory: dict = state.data.setdefault("panes", {})
    incidents: dict = state.data.setdefault("pane_incidents", {})
    if not panes and (memory or incidents):
        # An empty pane list is the other absence that is not evidence. `tmux list-panes`
        # answers "no server running" both when the server really went away and when this
        # process cannot reach its socket -- a wrong TMUX_TMPDIR, a mise/system tmux version
        # mismatch. Clearing on it would send "the pane no longer exists" for every open
        # incident and forget every pane this watch has learnt, on a run that read nothing.
        # A run that saw panes before and sees none now holds instead, and says so.
        log(
            f"panes: no pane at all, but this watch remembers {len(memory)} and holds"
            f" {len(incidents)} open incident(s); holding rather than clearing on an empty read"
            f" (TMUX_TMPDIR={os.environ.get('TMUX_TMPDIR', '(unset)')})"
        )
        return 0, 0, 0, 0
    try:
        children, info = process_table()
    except StepCut as cut:
        log(f"panes: not checked this run: {cut}")
        return 0, 0, 0, 0
    pid_session = {pid: session for session, pid in held.items()}
    seen_keys = set()
    agents = at_shell = alerts = clears = 0

    def clear(key: str, incident: dict, cause: str) -> None:
        nonlocal clears
        if incident.get("alerted_at") and not incident.get("cleared_at"):
            incident["cleared_at"] = now
            state.commit(options)
            head = (
                f"AGENT PANE AT SHELL CLEARED: tmux pane {incident['pane_id']}"
                f" ({incident['where']}). What cleared it: {cause}."
            )
            text, report = compose(
                options, f"pane-{incident['pane_id'].strip('%')}-cleared",
                guard_verdict(
                    f"{head} The incident opened at {iso(incident['opened_at'])} and lasted"
                    f" {human(now - incident['opened_at'])}. No action is needed. From"
                    f" envoy-role-holder-watch on {socket.gethostname()}."
                ),
                now, head,
                f"It opened {iso(incident['opened_at'])} and lasted"
                f" {human(now - incident['opened_at'])}. No action is needed.",
            )
            targets = incident.get("told") or [named_recipient(INFRA_LEAD, sessions)]
            told = [target for target in targets if options.dry_run or envoy.send(target, text)]
            clears += 1
            log(f"pane {incident['pane_id']} {incident['where']}: CLEARED ({cause});"
                f" {'would tell' if options.dry_run else 'told'} {len(told)}/{len(targets)} sessions;"
                f" full report {report}")
            if options.print_alert:
                print(text)
        incidents.pop(key, None)

    def at_shell_pane(
        pane: dict, key: str, logged: str, where: str, pane_pid: int, comm: str,
        remembered: dict | None, incident: dict | None,
    ) -> None:
        """The rest of the check for an agent pane at a bare shell: resolve its session, then
        hold, release or alert. Every read in it is a bounded step, and one that is cut raises
        StepCut before anything is decided for the pane."""
        nonlocal at_shell, alerts
        text = title_text(pane["pane_title"])
        # Resolve the session this pane last ran: what this watch saw running here, else the
        # transcript in its directory titled as the pane is, else the newest resurrect save.
        if incident is None or "session_source" not in incident:
            session = restart = transcript = None
            source = ""
            if remembered and remembered.get("session"):
                session = remembered["session"]
                source = f"seen running in this pane by this watch at {iso(remembered.get('seen_at'))}"
            else:
                found = transcript_by_title(pane["pane_current_path"], text, held)
                if found:
                    session, transcript = found[0], str(found[1])
                    source = f"the transcript in the pane's directory whose title is in the pane title, {found[1].name}"
                else:
                    saved = resurrect_command(pane, text)
                    if saved:
                        restart = saved[0]
                        session, transcript = resume_target(saved[0])
                        source = (
                            f"the resume command tmux-resurrect saved for {where} in {saved[1]}"
                            + ("" if session else
                               " -- which names a path whose header this watch could not read,"
                               " so the session is NOT resolved")
                        )
            incident = incidents.setdefault(key, {"opened_at": now})
            transcript = transcript or (str(transcript_of(session) or "") if session and UUID_RE.fullmatch(session) else "")
            exists, last_write = file_state(transcript) if transcript else (False, None)
            if transcript and not exists:
                # A path a resume command named that is not there is not a transcript; keeping
                # it would put a `grep -F <path>` in the alert that can never match and reads
                # to a person as "nothing holds it".
                transcript = ""
            # `omp --resume <id>` resolves an id against the session store of the directory it
            # is run in, and a pane's cwd is not always the directory the session was started
            # in: measured 2026-11-01 17:51Z on pane %58, where `omp --resume
            # 01a19827-…` run from /home/sami/Code/legion answered `Error: Session
            # "01a19827-…" not found.` while the session's transcript sat under
            # -src-oh-my-pi. `--resume <path>` takes the transcript itself and needs no
            # directory, so it is what the alert hands a reader whenever the transcript lives
            # outside the pane's own session directory.
            by_id = f"omp --resume {session}" if session else ""
            own_dir = str(session_dir_of(pane["pane_current_path"]))
            if transcript and not str(Path(transcript).parent).startswith(own_dir):
                by_id = f"omp --resume {shlex.quote(transcript)}"
            incident.update({
                "pane_id": pane["pane_id"], "where": where, "window_name": pane["window_name"],
                "title": pane["pane_title"], "cwd": pane["pane_current_path"], "shell": comm,
                "shell_pid": pane_pid, "session": session, "session_source": source,
                "transcript": transcript,
                "restart": restart or by_id,
            })
            incident["last_write"] = last_write if transcript else None
        # EVERY period, not only the one that opened the incident. The pane being at a shell is
        # a fact about the pane; whether its session is running somewhere else is a fact about
        # the session, and that one can change between the open and the 180 s alert. Checking it
        # once meant an alert could name a session that was live at the moment it was sent, and
        # hand a reader the send-keys that starts a second omp on its transcript -- the
        # INFRA-681 double loop the same alert warns about.
        session = incident.get("session")
        if session and session in held:
            log_change(
                state, logged, f"elsewhere:{session}:{held[session]}",
                f"pane {pane['pane_id']} {where}: at a shell, but its session {session} runs in"
                f" pid {held[session]} elsewhere; not an incident",
            )
            clear(key, incident, f"its session {session} runs in pid {held[session]} elsewhere")
            memory.setdefault(key, {})["left"] = {"session": session, "pid": held[session], "at": now}
            incidents.pop(key, None)
            state.commit(options)
            return
        released = pane_release(memory.get(key) or {}, session, incident.get("transcript") or "")
        if released:
            log_change(
                state, logged, f"released:{released}",
                f"pane {pane['pane_id']} {where}: at a shell, but {released}; not an incident",
            )
            clear(key, incident, released)
            state.commit(options)
            return
        at_shell += 1
        open_for = now - incident["opened_at"]
        if open_for < options.pane_grace:
            log_change(
                state, logged, f"grace:{incident['opened_at']}",
                f"pane {pane['pane_id']} {where}: at a shell since {iso(incident['opened_at'])}"
                f" (< {int(options.pane_grace)} s grace), holding",
            )
            state.commit(options)
            return
        # A pane in a tmux session named mv-<worker> is `ossv-agent move` relocating that worker
        # to another box. Its pane shows the worker's `[Stopped by SIGTERM]` and the mover's own
        # recovery lines, which is exactly the shape this check fires on -- and telling anyone to
        # resume it would start a second omp on a session the mover is about to resume elsewhere.
        # Measured 2026-10-30: three such alerts between 20:14Z and 20:35Z on ossv-agent-open.
        # It is held, not dropped: a move that never finishes is a real failure, so after
        # --move-grace the alert goes out saying a move is in progress and where the worker is,
        # and never how to restart it.
        move = move_in_progress(pane)
        if move:
            incident["move"] = move
            if open_for < options.move_grace:
                log_change(
                    state, logged, f"move:{move['worker']}:{incident['opened_at']}",
                    f"pane {pane['pane_id']} {where}: move in progress (mover session"
                    f" {move['mover']}, worker {move['worker']}), at a shell since"
                    f" {iso(incident['opened_at'])} (< {int(options.move_grace)} s move grace);"
                    " not an incident",
                )
                state.commit(options)
                return
        if incident.get("alerted_at"):
            log_change(
                state, logged, f"alerted:{incident['alerted_at']}",
                f"pane {pane['pane_id']} {where}: at a shell, alerted at"
                f" {iso(incident['alerted_at'])}; nothing further goes out for this incident",
            )
            return
        # One alert per pane per session until omp runs in the pane again, however often the
        # incident closes and reopens in between (a shell child that comes and goes, a session
        # seen elsewhere and gone again): the lane's state has not changed, so neither has the
        # alert a reader already has.
        record = memory.setdefault(key, {})
        if record.get("alerted_for") == (session or "-"):
            log_change(
                state, logged, f"realerted:{record.get('alerted_for')}:{record.get('alerted_at')}",
                f"pane {pane['pane_id']} {where}: at a shell again; this watch alerted on it at"
                f" {iso(record.get('alerted_at'))} for session {session or '(not resolved)'}, and"
                " says nothing more until omp runs in this pane again",
            )
            return
        session = incident.get("session")
        directory = pane["pane_current_path"]
        if incident.get("transcript"):
            directory = session_cwd(incident["transcript"]) or directory
        incident["handover"] = handover_files(session if session and UUID_RE.fullmatch(session) else "-", directory)
        targets, notes = pane_recipients(directory, session, sessions)
        incident["notes"] = notes
        # Read now, not when the incident opened: whether a transcript's session is running is
        # a fact about this moment, and the alert says which of them are.
        if not session:
            incident["directory_inventory"] = directory_inventory(pane["pane_current_path"], held)
        # Named in the alert, so a reader knows who else saw it and under which role.
        incident["told_why"] = [f"{why} {target}" for target, why in targets]
        full = move_alert_text(incident, now) if incident.get("move") else pane_alert_text(incident, now)
        # Where a reader already looks: a suppression of another class is named in this one, so
        # nobody has to read the source of the watch to learn that a class has been silenced.
        full = "\n".join([full] + suppression_lines(now, state.data.get("suppressed") or {}))
        text, report = compose(
            options, f"pane-{pane['pane_id'].strip('%')}-{'move' if incident.get('move') else 'at-shell'}",
            full, now, *pane_head(incident, now),
        )
        incident["alerted_at"] = now
        incident["told"] = []
        record.update({"alerted_for": session or "-", "alerted_at": now})
        log_change(state, logged, f"alerted:{now}")
        state.commit(options)
        told = []
        for target, why in targets:
            if options.dry_run or envoy.send(target, text):
                told.append(target)
        incident["told"] = told
        state.commit(options)
        alerts += 1
        log(f"pane {pane['pane_id']} {where}: ALERT {'would send' if options.dry_run else 'sent'}"
            f" to {len(told)}/{len(targets)} sessions"
            f" [{', '.join(told)}]; full report {report}")
        if options.print_alert:
            print(text)

    for pane in panes:
        check_time("the pane check")
        key = f"{pane['pid']}.{pane['start_time']}:{pane['pane_id']}"
        logged = f"pane:{key}"
        seen_keys.add(key)
        where = f"{pane['session_name']}:{pane['window_index']}.{pane['pane_index']}"
        pane_pid = int(pane["pane_pid"] or 0)
        stack, omp_pids = [pane_pid], []
        while stack:
            pid = stack.pop()
            if info.get(pid, ("", 0))[0] == "omp":
                omp_pids.append(pid)
            stack.extend(children.get(pid, []))
        remembered = memory.get(key)
        if not (pane["pane_title"].startswith("π ") or remembered or omp_pids):
            continue
        agents += 1
        incident = incidents.get(key)
        if omp_pids:
            facts = [_OMP_PIDS.get(p) or {} for p in omp_pids]
            session = next((fact["primary"] for fact in facts if fact.get("primary")), None) or next(
                (pid_session[p] for p in omp_pids if p in pid_session), None
            )
            record = memory.setdefault(key, {})
            record.update({"where": where, "title": pane["pane_title"], "cwd": pane["pane_current_path"], "seen_at": now})
            if session:
                record["session"] = session
            # Every session the pane's omp holds, so a move of the one it runs is matched even
            # when the top-level one could not be told; and whether it is a one-shot.
            record["sessions"] = sorted(s for s, p in held.items() if p in omp_pids)[:8]
            record["oneshot"] = any(fact.get("oneshot") for fact in facts)
            # omp runs in this pane again: its next stop is news, whatever came before.
            for stale in ("left", "alerted_for"):
                record.pop(stale, None)
            if incident:
                clear(key, incident, f"omp (pid {omp_pids[0]}) runs in the pane again"
                      + (f", session {session}" if session else ""))
            # A healthy pane has never had a line of its own, and the summary counts it.
            log_change(state, logged, f"omp:{session or omp_pids[0]}")
            continue
        comm, tpgid = info.get(pane_pid, ("", 0))
        if not (comm in SHELLS and tpgid == pane_pid):
            log_change(
                state, logged, f"busy:{pane['pane_current_command']}",
                f"pane {pane['pane_id']} {where}: agent pane runs {pane['pane_current_command']},"
                " not omp and not a bare shell; holding",
            )
            continue
        # A pane whose shell has a live child is working, whatever its foreground process group
        # says. A script started with `&` or left running by a wrapper sits outside the pane's
        # foreground group, so tpgid reads back as the shell itself and the pane looks idle.
        # Measured 2026-11-01 18:40Z on pane %724: the alert fired while the shell's child was
        # `gate-then-merge.sh bet 2322 …`, and the `tmux send-keys` it recommended would have
        # started an omp beside a running gate. The children are named rather than counted, so a
        # reader can tell a gate run from a stray `less`.
        kids = [p for p in children.get(pane_pid, []) if p in info]
        if kids:
            names = ", ".join(f"{info[p][0]} (pid {p})" for p in sorted(kids)[:3])
            log_change(
                state, logged, f"children:{names}",
                f"pane {pane['pane_id']} {where}: at a shell, but its shell has live children:"
                f" {names}; holding",
            )
            if incident:
                clear(key, incident, f"its shell has live children: {names}")
            continue
        if pane.get("@agent_oneshot", "").strip() not in ("", "0", "false", "off"):
            # Its owner says this pane runs a one-shot omp and outlives it, so a shell here is the
            # end of the work, not the death of a lane. An incident already open is cleared rather
            # than left to sit: the option may have been set after the first alert.
            log_change(
                state, logged, "oneshot",
                f"pane {pane['pane_id']} {where}: at a shell, @agent_oneshot set by its owner;"
                " not an incident",
            )
            if incident:
                clear(key, incident, "its owner marks this pane @agent_oneshot: a one-shot omp that exits by design")
            continue
        # The box says how its omp ended, in the pane, and a clean end is not an incident. On
        # 2026-11-06 pane %1907 alerted at 20:23Z while its own last line read
        #   agentbox-...: omp (pid 1621) exited after 20732s (exit code 0, signal none); the box ends
        # and its issue (LINUX-1288) was done: the lane had finished, and two leads each spent a
        # round trip reading a pane to find that out. The transcript tests above miss this
        # because a boxed session's exit is recorded by the box, not as a session_exit this host
        # can read. Only 0 with no signal is release: pane %37 on the same day ended
        # `exit code 137, signal SIGKILL`, which is a lane earlyoom killed and is exactly what
        # this watch is for.
        ended = box_exit_code(pane["pane_id"])
        if ended and ended == (0, "none"):
            log_change(
                state, logged, "box-exit-clean",
                f"pane {pane['pane_id']} {where}: at a shell, its box reports omp exit code 0"
                " with no signal; the lane finished",
            )
            if incident:
                clear(key, incident, "its box reports omp exited with code 0 and no signal: the lane finished")
            continue
        # A pane whose session was seen running in a process outside it is no longer that lane's
        # pane: the lane went elsewhere, and where it runs is watched there. Until omp runs in
        # this pane again it is a leftover shell. On 2026-11-03 pane %40 (session 01a19366)
        # alerted at 06:54Z and 07:09Z and cleared at 06:48Z and 07:04Z: each time a process was
        # read as its session running elsewhere the incident closed, and each time that process
        # stopped the pane opened a new one. (Those processes were annotation runs that only
        # named the session; `runs_no_session` now keeps them out, and this rule keeps one alert.)
        left = (memory.get(key) or {}).get("left")
        if left:
            log_change(
                state, logged, f"left:{left.get('session')}",
                f"pane {pane['pane_id']} {where}: at a shell; its session {left.get('session')} ran"
                f" in pid {left.get('pid')} outside this pane at {iso(left.get('at'))}, so nothing"
                " is watched here until omp runs in this pane again",
            )
            if incident:
                clear(key, incident, f"its session {left.get('session')} runs outside this pane")
            continue
        try:
            at_shell_pane(pane, key, logged, where, pane_pid, comm, remembered, incident)
        except StepCut as cut:
            # Nothing was decided for this pane -- no alert, no clear -- and its journal state
            # stays as it was. The next period reads it again.
            _LOG_KEYS.add(logged)
            log(f"pane {pane['pane_id']} {where}: not judged this run: {cut}")

    for key in list(incidents):
        if key not in seen_keys:
            clear(key, incidents[key], "the pane no longer exists (closed, or its tmux server restarted)")
    for key in list(memory):
        if key not in seen_keys:
            memory.pop(key)
    # Only here, where every pane of this server has been read: a pane the run never reached
    # keeps its recorded journal state, so the next run does not log it again as news.
    forget_logged(state, "pane:")
    state.commit(options)
    return agents, at_shell, alerts, clears



def keep_known_transcripts(state: State) -> None:
    """Put into the state file the known transcripts of the sessions it still watches: every
    role holder, and the session of every pane it remembers or holds an incident on. The next
    run reads them back into `_KNOWN_TRANSCRIPTS`, so a holder that stops after this run is
    looked up with one stat, not a walk of the session root. The same for what the session sweep
    did with a watched session's transcript (`_SWEPT`), so the table is read once per holder."""
    watched = {record.get("holder") for record in (state.data.get("roles") or {}).values()}
    for group in ("panes", "pane_incidents"):
        watched |= {record.get("session") for record in (state.data.get(group) or {}).values()
                    if isinstance(record, dict)}
    state.data["transcripts"] = {
        session: path for session, path in sorted(_KNOWN_TRANSCRIPTS.items()) if session in watched
    }
    state.data["swept"] = {
        session: record for session, record in sorted(_SWEPT.items()) if session in watched
    }


def check_roles(
    options: argparse.Namespace, state: State, envoy: Envoy, sessions: list[dict],
    live: dict[str, dict], held: dict[str, int], roles_state: dict, incidents: dict,
    suppressed: dict, pane_of: dict[str, str], host: str, started: float,
    local_names: set[str] | None = None,
) -> tuple[int, int]:
    """(alerts, clears) for every Envoy role whose holder session is on this machine.

    It raises OutOfTime when the run's budget runs out between two roles: what it has decided
    so far is in the state file, and the roles it did not reach are the ones the next run
    starts with.
    """
    alerts = clears = 0
    local_names = local_names or {short_host(host)}
    remote_results: dict[str, tuple[str, dict[str, str]]] = {}

    for role in sorted(roles_state, key=lambda name: ((roles_state[name] or {}).get("checked_at") or 0, name)):
        check_time("the role checks")
        record = roles_state[role]
        holder = record.get("holder")
        if not holder or short_host(record.get("host") or "") not in local_names:
            continue
        record["checked_at"] = started
        key = f"{role}|{holder}"
        logged = f"role:{role}"
        incident = incidents.get(key)

        # A named class suppression, checked before any work is done for the role: see
        # SUPPRESSIONS. It sends nothing for this role, counts itself, and closes an incident
        # the class had already alerted on, so a reader is not left holding an open alert that
        # will never be mentioned again.
        rule = suppression_for(role, started)
        if rule:
            counted = suppressed.setdefault(rule["shown"], {"count": 0})
            if not record.get("suppressed_at"):
                record["suppressed_at"] = started
                counted["count"] = counted.get("count", 0) + 1
                counted["role"] = role
                counted["at"] = started
                log(
                    f"{role}: SUPPRESSED by \"{rule['name']}\" ({rule['shown']}) until"
                    f" {rule['until']}; nothing is sent for this holder, and the suppression is"
                    f" named in every other alert ({counted['count']} role(s) so far)"
                )
            if incident and send_clear(
                envoy, options, role, incident, holder,
                f"the suppression \"{rule['name']}\" covers {rule['shown']} until"
                f" {rule['until']}",
                started, "suppressed", suppression_lines(started, suppressed),
            ):
                clears += 1
            if started - (record.get("suppressed_at") or started) > SUPPRESSED_FORGET:
                # Pure bookkeeping, and it has to happen: this class claims a new role every
                # couple of minutes and up to a thousand in one capacity test, and the state
                # file is rewritten and fsynced every 30 s.
                incidents.pop(key, None)
                roles_state.pop(role, None)
            state.commit(options)
            continue

        # A remembered host is not ownership: the same session id can move to another host.
        entry = live.get(holder)
        registry_result = None
        if entry is None:
            registry_result = envoy.registry(holder)
            if registry_result[0] == 200:
                entry = registry_result[1]
                live[holder] = entry
        where = session_host(entry) if entry else ""
        if where and where not in local_names:
            cause = f"holder registered on {where}. This host skips it. Its host's watch judges it"
            if incident and send_clear(envoy, options, role, incident, holder, cause, started, "elsewhere"):
                clears += 1
            log(f"{role}: skipping {holder}: {cause}")
            incidents.pop(key, None)
            roles_state.pop(role, None)
            state.commit(options)
            continue
        # A holder the last walk missed is walked for again only after TRANSCRIPT_REWALK.
        rewalk = started - (record.get("transcript_missed_at") or 0) >= TRANSCRIPT_REWALK
        # An incident that is finished -- a recorded normal exit, or an alert quieted on the
        # directory rule -- has one question left, "did the session come back", and the two free
        # sources answer it. The deep sources are what cost the run of 2026-11-03T00:18:07Z 80 s
        # on a holder that had exited the day before and whose transcript the sweep had taken.
        finished = bool(incident) and bool(
            incident.get("quieted_at") or (incident.get("exited_at") and incident.get("exited_reported_at"))
        )
        try:
            evidence = holder_evidence(
                envoy, holder, held, live, options.write_grace, started,
                record.get("dir") or "", options.dir_grace, rewalk, not finished,
                registry_result,
            )
        except StepCut as cut:
            # A source this role needed was not read: nothing is decided for it -- no alert, no
            # clear -- and its journal state stays as it was. The next period reads it again,
            # after the roles this one has now been checked behind (`checked_at`).
            _LOG_KEYS.update({logged, f"{logged}:registry"})
            log(f"{role}: holder {holder} not judged this run: {cut}")
            continue
        if evidence["walk_missed"]:
            record["transcript_missed_at"] = started
        pid = evidence["pid"]
        # A holder whose only sighting would have been a transcript header this run had no time
        # to read is not unseen, it is unread. Judging it would alert on a lane that is running.
        if not evidence["alive"] and _SCAN["unread_headers"]:
            log_change(
                state, logged, f"unread:{_SCAN['unread_headers']}",
                f"{role}: holding {holder}: {_SCAN['unread_headers']} transcript header(s) were"
                " left unread at this run's header budget, and one of them could name it",
            )
            continue

        # A role whose holder another live session has taken is not this holder's incident.
        taken_by = next(
            (
                session["session_id"]
                for session in sessions
                if session.get("session_id") != holder and role in (session.get("roles") or [])
            ),
            None,
        )

        # Disagreement between the process and the registry is a registry fault, never an alert:
        # under load Envoy drops sessions that are running (envoy_sessions omits them,
        # envoy_send answers "no live session"), which the Rocq speed lane recorded on
        # 2026-10-25 15:10-15:16Z and agent infra measured again at load 233 on 2026-10-29.
        if pid and evidence["registry"] != "live":
            log_change(
                state, f"{logged}:registry", f"fault:{holder}:{evidence['registry']}",
                f"{role}: {CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER} {holder} in Envoy's registry, but pid {pid} holds"
                " it -- registry fault, not an incident; no role lookup and no alert",
            )
        else:
            log_change(state, f"{logged}:registry", f"ok:{holder}:{evidence['registry']}")

        cause = ""
        if evidence["alive"]:
            if pid:
                record["last_proc_pid"] = pid
                record["last_proc_seen"] = started
            cause = f"this watch can see the holder again -- {evidence['found'] or 'a source'}"
        elif taken_by:
            cause = f"a new holder took the role: session {taken_by} now lists {role}"

        if cause:
            if incident and send_clear(envoy, options, role, incident, holder, cause, started):
                clears += 1
                state.commit(options)
            incidents.pop(key, None)
            if evidence["alive"]:
                # One line when a role becomes ok, none while it stays ok: 10,723 of the 23,597
                # journal lines of the six hours to 2026-11-03T02:36Z were this line repeating.
                log_change(
                    state, logged, f"ok:{holder}:{evidence['found_source']}:{pid}",
                    f"{role}: ok (holder {holder}, {evidence['found']})",
                )
            else:
                log(f"{role}: forgetting {holder} ({cause})")
                roles_state.pop(role, None)
            continue

        incident = incidents.setdefault(key, {})
        incident.setdefault("opened_at", started)
        incident["sources"] = [list(source) for source in evidence["sources"]]
        incident["verdict"] = evidence["verdict"]
        if "last_seen_alive" not in incident:
            # One deterministic value, read once when the incident opens: st_mtime of the
            # holder's transcript. Never the run's own clock -- two runs that open the same
            # incident at different seconds would disagree about when it was last written.
            incident["last_seen_alive"] = evidence["last_write"]
            incident["last_proc_pid"] = record.get("last_proc_pid")
            incident["last_pid_seen_at"] = record.get("last_proc_seen")
        unseen_for = started - incident["opened_at"]

        # A holder that ended ON PURPOSE is not an incident: its transcript's last entry is a
        # normal session_exit (see normal_exit_at). Said once -- in the journal, and to everyone
        # an earlier alert reached -- and then never again for this holder. The marker lives
        # on the incident, in the same state file as alerted_at, and is forgotten with it.
        #
        # The marker, not the transcript, is the record from then on. On 2026-11-02 the exit of
        # bet-owner-nickmine's holder was recorded at 05:47:45Z; at 13:20:57Z the run could not
        # re-read the transcript inside its walk budget, fell through to "not yet alerted" and
        # sent a first alert, and at 14:10:46Z, after the session sweep had deleted the file,
        # it escalated. A planned stop is not undone by its file moving. Only one thing undoes
        # it: the session writing again after its exit, which is a resume -- and a resumed
        # session that this watch sees running closes the incident, marker and all.
        exited = evidence["exited_at"] or incident.get("exited_at")
        exit_time = _entry_time(str(incident.get("exited_at") or ""))
        if (
            not evidence["exited_at"] and exit_time and evidence["transcript"]
            and (evidence["last_write"] or 0) > exit_time + EXIT_TAIL_S
        ):
            log(
                f"{role}: holder {holder} wrote {evidence['transcript']} at"
                f" {iso(evidence['last_write'])}, after its normal exit at {incident['exited_at']}:"
                " it was resumed, so that exit no longer counts"
            )
            for field in ("exited_at", "exited_reported_at"):
                incident.pop(field, None)
            exited = None
        if exited:
            late = bool(incident.get("alerted_at")) and not incident.get("cleared_at")
            if not incident.get("exited_reported_at") or late:
                recorded = incident.get("exited_reported_at")
                incident["exited_reported_at"] = recorded or started
                incident["exited_at"] = exited
                state.commit(options)
                if evidence["exited_at"] and evidence["transcript"]:
                    where = f"the last entry of {evidence['transcript']} is a normal session_exit"
                elif evidence["exited_at"] and evidence["swept"]:
                    where = (
                        f"the last entry of {evidence['swept']['path']}, read from Postgres"
                        " omp_sessions where the session sweep moved it, is a normal session_exit"
                    )
                else:
                    where = (
                        f"this watch recorded that exit at {iso(recorded)}, and the transcript is"
                        " not readable now"
                    )
                said = f"holder exited normally at {exited}"
                if late and recorded:
                    said += (
                        f"; the alert of {iso(incident['alerted_at'])} about it was wrong: it went"
                        f" out after this watch had recorded that exit at {iso(recorded)}, when a"
                        " later run could not re-read the transcript"
                    )
                log(f"{role}: {said} (session {holder}; {where}); not alerting on this holder again")
                if send_clear(
                    envoy, options, role, incident, holder, said, started, "exited",
                    suppression_lines(started, suppressed),
                ):
                    clears += 1
            elif unseen_for > FORGET_AFTER:
                incidents.pop(key, None)
                roles_state.pop(role, None)
            else:
                log_change(state, logged, f"exited:{exited}")
            state.commit(options)
            continue

        if unseen_for < options.grace:
            log_change(
                state, logged, f"holding:{holder}:{incident['opened_at']}",
                f"{role}: {CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER} {holder} since {iso(incident['opened_at'])}"
                f" (< {options.grace} s grace), holding",
            )
            state.commit(options)
            continue

        # THE DIRECTORY RULE. An incident this watch has no source for can repeat until the
        # lane ends, and nothing it reads will ever clear it. One thing can: a named file in
        # the holder's own working directory, written inside --dir-grace, dated absolutely.
        #
        # What it is allowed to conclude, and nothing more: a reader cannot answer this alert.
        # Looking for a pane, a transcript and a process has already failed on all four
        # sources, and the lane's directory is being written by something that is not any of
        # them. It does NOT conclude that the lane works -- see clear_text("quiet").
        #
        # It never holds back a FIRST alert for a role whose directory went quiet with its
        # holder: that is the 2026-10-27T05:52Z shape this watch exists for. The cost it does
        # accept is the other way round -- a real loss in a directory other lanes write gets
        # its first alert and then one quieting message instead of an escalation. That trade is
        # deliberate and it is the only part of this rule that could hide a real failure.
        if incident.get("quieted_at"):
            log_change(
                state, logged, f"quieted:{incident['quieted_at']}",
                f"{role}: {CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER} {holder}, quieted at"
                f" {iso(incident['quieted_at'])} on the directory rule; nothing further goes out"
                " for this holder",
            )
            if unseen_for > FORGET_AFTER:
                incidents.pop(key, None)
                roles_state.pop(role, None)
            state.commit(options)
            continue

        if evidence["dir_fresh"]:
            incident["quieted_at"] = started
            incident["quiet_file"] = evidence["dir_file"]
            incident["quiet_write"] = evidence["dir_write"]
            state.commit(options)
            if send_clear(
                envoy, options, role, incident, holder, evidence["dir_sentence"], started,
                "quiet", suppression_lines(started, suppressed),
            ):
                clears += 1
            else:
                log(
                    f"{role}: NOT ALERTING on {holder}: no source can see the holder, and"
                    f" {evidence['dir_sentence']}"
                )
            state.commit(options)
            continue

        remote = {}
        if evidence["registry"] == "absent":
            if holder not in remote_results:
                remote_results[holder] = remote_presence(
                    holder, host, getattr(options, "check_hosts", DEFAULT_CHECK_HOSTS),
                )
            found_host, remote = remote_results[holder]
            if found_host:
                cause = f"omp-session-live found the holder on {found_host}; do not resume it here"
                if send_clear(envoy, options, role, incident, holder, cause, started):
                    clears += 1
                log(f"{role}: {cause}")
                incidents.pop(key, None)
                state.commit(options)
                continue
        incident["hosts_checked"] = checked_hosts(host, remote)
        incident["remote_checks"] = remote

        if not incident.get("alerted_at"):
            incident["transcript"] = evidence["transcript"]
            incident["swept"] = evidence["swept"]
            try:
                incident["handover"] = handover_files(holder, record.get("dir") or "")
            except StepCut as cut:
                # The alert goes out whole or not at all: the next period sends it.
                _LOG_KEYS.add(logged)
                log(f"{role}: alert on {holder} held to the next run: {cut}")
                continue
            incident["listener"] = envoy.url
            incident["pane"] = pane_of.get(holder, "")
            targets, topics, notes = recipients(role, record, sessions, holder)
            incident["notes"] = notes
            for field in ("dir", "title", "host"):
                incident[field] = record.get(field)
            kind = "first alert"
            full = alert_text(
                role, incident, holder, started, kind, suppression_lines(started, suppressed),
            )
            full += "\n" + "\n".join(f"{name}: {result}" for name, result in remote.items())
            text, report = compose(
                options, f"role-{role}-alert", full, started,
                *alert_head(role, incident, holder, started, kind),
                *suppression_note(started),
                required=(incident["hosts_checked"],),
            )
            # On disk before a single message leaves: a resent first alert is worse than a late one.
            incident["alerted_at"] = started
            incident["told"] = []
            log_change(state, logged, f"alerted:{started}")
            state.commit(options)
            told = []
            for target, why in targets:
                if options.dry_run or envoy.send(target, text):
                    told.append(target)
            published = 0
            for topic in topics:
                headline = short_message(
                    report,
                    f"ENVOY ROLE HOLDER UNSEEN: no source has seen the holder of role {role}"
                    f" ({holder}) since {iso(incident['opened_at'])}, so a message to"
                    f" notifications.role.{role} may reach nobody. This watch cannot see that"
                    " session; it does NOT establish that it stopped.",
                    required=(incident["hosts_checked"], next_reading(started)),
                )
                if options.dry_run or envoy.publish(topic, headline):
                    published += 1
            incident["told"] = told
            state.commit(options)
            alerts += 1
            log(f"{role}: ALERT {'would send' if options.dry_run else 'sent'} to {len(told)}/{len(targets)} session(s)"
                f" [{', '.join(f'{t} ({w})' for t, w in targets if t in told)}] and {published}/{len(topics)} topic(s);"
                f" full report {report}")
            if options.print_alert:
                print(text)
        elif (
            started - incident["alerted_at"] >= options.escalate
            and not incident.get("escalated_at")
        ):
            # The one repeat an incident gets, and it says so: the facts are the ones the first
            # alert carried, so the message names when the incident was first seen and first
            # alerted, how long ago that really was, and that nothing further will follow.
            first_seen, first_alert = iso(incident["opened_at"]), iso(incident["alerted_at"])
            kind = (
                f"REPEAT of the incident first seen at {first_seen},"
                f" {human(started - incident['alerted_at'])} after its first alert"
            )
            full = alert_text(
                role, incident, holder, started, kind, suppression_lines(started, suppressed),
            )
            full += (
                f"\nTHIS IS A REPEAT, AND THE ONLY ONE: the incident was first seen at {first_seen}"
                f" and first alerted at {first_alert}, and no source has seen the holder since."
                " No further alert goes out for this holder unless a source sees it again or its"
                " transcript is found to end in a normal exit. You own looking at the pane."
            )
            text, report = compose(
                options, f"role-{role}-repeat", full, started,
                f"ENVOY ROLE HOLDER UNSEEN: {role} ({holder}). REPEAT of the incident first seen"
                f" at {first_seen}. First alerted at {first_alert}. This is"
                f" {human(started - incident['alerted_at'])} after its first alert, and the only"
                " repeat. This does not prove the holder stopped.",
                required=(incident["hosts_checked"],),
            )
            incident["escalated_at"] = started
            log_change(state, logged, f"escalated:{started}")
            state.commit(options)
            target = named_recipient(CHIEF_OF_STAFF, sessions)
            confirmed = options.dry_run or envoy.send(target, text)
            outcome = "would send" if options.dry_run else ("sent" if confirmed else "UNCONFIRMED")
            log(
                f"{role}: {'ESCALATED' if confirmed else 'ESCALATION UNCONFIRMED'} ({outcome}) to the chief of staff ({target}), a repeat of the incident first seen at"
                f" {first_seen} and first alerted at {first_alert}; no further repeat;"
                f" full report {report}"
            )
        elif unseen_for > FORGET_AFTER:
            log(
                f"{role}: dropping {holder} from the state file after {human(unseen_for)} unseen;"
                " it was alerted and escalated, and nothing re-reads it"
            )
            incidents.pop(key, None)
            roles_state.pop(role, None)
        else:
            log_change(
                state, logged, f"alerted:{incident.get('alerted_at')}",
                f"{role}: {CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER} {holder}, alerted at"
                f" {iso(incident.get('alerted_at'))}; nothing further goes out until a source"
                " sees it or 10 minutes pass",
            )
        state.commit(options)

    return alerts, clears


# A run that starts with its budget already spent still does one unit of work per phase, so
# every period moves at least one role forward. A run that did nothing at all would be a watch
# that has stopped watching while systemd and the timer both look healthy, which is the failure
# this file exists for.
MIN_RUN_SECONDS = 1.0


def run_once(options: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    """One period. Its disk and /proc steps run bounded only inside it (see bounded_step)."""
    try:
        return run_period(options)
    finally:
        _STEPS.update(bounded=False, lock_fd=None)


def run_period(options: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    global _TRANSCRIPT_INDEX, _OPEN_JSONL, _SWEEP_INDEX, _PROC_TABLE
    started = time.time()
    budget = getattr(options, "budget", None) or DEFAULT_BUDGET
    _RUN["deadline"] = max(process_started() + budget, started + MIN_RUN_SECONDS)
    # Where each run spends its time, in the summary line, so a run that nears its
    # TimeoutStartSec names the step that took it there.
    phases: list[str] = []
    mark = started

    def phase(name: str) -> None:
        nonlocal mark
        phases.append(f"{name} {time.time() - mark:.1f}s")
        mark = time.time()

    envoy = Envoy(timeout=options.http_timeout)
    state = State(options.state)
    if not state.locked:
        log(f"another run holds {options.state}; skipping this period")
        return 0
    # From here every disk and /proc step runs in a child under a hard limit (bounded_step).
    _STEPS.update(bounded=True, child=False, lock_fd=state.handle.fileno(), cut=[])
    _TRANSCRIPT_INDEX = _OPEN_JSONL = _SWEEP_INDEX = _PROC_TABLE = None
    _TRANSCRIPT_WALK.update(deadline=0.0, truncated=False)
    _LOG_KEYS.clear()
    _SCAN["unread_headers"] = 0
    load_header_cache(Path(options.state).parent / "headers.json")
    _KNOWN_TRANSCRIPTS.clear()
    _KNOWN_TRANSCRIPTS.update(
        {session: path for session, path in (state.data.get("transcripts") or {}).items()
         if isinstance(path, str)}
    )
    _SWEPT.clear()
    _SWEPT.update(
        {session: record for session, record in (state.data.get("swept") or {}).items()
         if isinstance(record, dict)}
    )
    roles_state: dict = state.data.setdefault("roles", {})
    # Incident memory, keyed by role AND holder so a role that is re-claimed by a new session
    # starts a new incident. It lives in this one file, which the service owns, and is written
    # and flushed to disk BEFORE any message goes out, so a crash between the two can never
    # resend a first alert.
    incidents: dict = state.data.setdefault("incidents", {})
    host = os.environ.get("AGENTBOX_HOST") or socket.gethostname()

    try:
        sessions = envoy.sessions()
    except RuntimeError as error:
        log(f"envoy unreachable: {error}")
        return 2
    phase("envoy")
    live = {
        session["session_id"]: session for session in sessions if session.get("session_id")
    }
    local_names = local_hostnames(host, sessions)
    local = [
        session
        for session in sessions
        if session_host(session) in local_names and session.get("roles")
    ]
    # Every step from here can run out of the run's budget, and each raises OutOfTime between
    # two units of work rather than inside one. A half-read process table would make live
    # holders look unseen, so a step that stops leaves nothing behind: the state file keeps what
    # whole steps decided, the summary line says where the run stopped, and the next period
    # starts with the roles this one did not reach.
    agents = at_shell = pane_alerts = pane_clears = alerts = clears = 0
    held: dict[str, int] = {}
    suppressed: dict = state.data.setdefault("suppressed", {})
    stopped = ""
    try:
        held = omp_sessions_by_pid()
    except OutOfTime as where:
        stopped = str(where)
    phase("processes")
    # Where this watch last saw each session running, for the alert's "look at the pane" step.
    pane_of: dict[str, str] = {}
    for pane_key, remembered in (state.data.get("panes") or {}).items():
        if isinstance(remembered, dict) and remembered.get("session"):
            pane_of[remembered["session"]] = (
                f"{pane_key.rsplit(':', 1)[-1]} ({remembered.get('where') or '?'},"
                f" {remembered.get('cwd') or '?'})"
            )

    # Learn the roles this machine holds, and keep what an earlier run learnt: a holder whose
    # session registration has already aged out is exactly the case this watch must still see.
    for session in local:
        for role in session.get("roles") or []:
            record = roles_state.setdefault(role, {})
            previous = record.get("holder")
            if previous != session["session_id"]:
                record.clear()
                record["holder"] = session["session_id"]
                # The old holder's incident, if any, is over: the role has moved.
                incidents.pop(f"{role}|{previous}", None)
            record.update(
                {
                    "dir": session.get("dir") or "",
                    "title": session.get("title") or "",
                    "host": host,
                    "topics": session.get("topics") or [],
                }
            )
    # On disk before any per-holder work: the roster and where each holder's transcript is, so a
    # run that is killed later still leaves the next one what this one learnt.
    keep_known_transcripts(state)
    state.commit(options)

    if not stopped:
        try:
            # The pane check first: it is cheap (0.2-1.3 s at load 400) and reads no holder, so
            # a period whose holder checks run long still checks every pane and its alerts.
            agents, at_shell, pane_alerts, pane_clears = check_panes(
                options, state, envoy, sessions, held, started
            )
            phase("panes")
            alerts, clears = check_roles(
                options, state, envoy, sessions, live, held, roles_state, incidents, suppressed,
                pane_of, host, started, local_names,
            )
        except OutOfTime as where:
            stopped = str(where)
    if "panes" not in " ".join(phases):
        phase("panes")
    phase("roles")
    # Only a run that reached every role may forget a role's recorded journal state: a run that
    # stopped, at its budget or on a cut process scan, would make the next one log every role again.
    if not stopped:
        forget_logged(state, "role:")
    keep_known_transcripts(state)
    state.commit(options)
    state.close()
    if not options.dry_run or getattr(options, "write_state", False):
        save_header_cache(Path(options.state).parent / "headers.json")
    # ONE line a run, whatever happened: the counts, each phase's seconds, what ran out, and the
    # suppressions in their short form. Everything else this run logs is a state that changed.
    log(
        f"checked {len(roles_state)} local role(s) and {agents} agent pane(s), {at_shell} at a shell,"
        f" in {time.time() - started:.2f}s ({', '.join(phases)}"
        + (f"; session-root walk stopped at its {TRANSCRIPT_WALK_SECONDS:g} s budget"
           if _TRANSCRIPT_WALK["truncated"] else "")
        + (f"; {_SCAN['unread_headers']} transcript header(s) left for the next run"
           if _SCAN["unread_headers"] else "")
        + f"); {alerts + pane_alerts} alert(s), {clears + pane_clears} clear(s)"
        + (f"; {len(_STEPS['cut'])} read(s) cut at their limit, nothing decided on them:"
           f" {', '.join(_STEPS['cut'])}" if _STEPS["cut"] else "")
        + (f"; STOPPED at its {budget:g} s budget in {stopped}, with what it had done saved: the"
           " next run starts with the roles this one did not reach" if stopped else "")
        + "".join("; " + line for line in suppression_lines(started, suppressed, short=True))
    )
    return 0


def main() -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    parser.add_argument("--grace", type=float, default=60.0, help="seconds every source must stay silent before a role alerts")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--write-grace",
        type=float,
        default=DEFAULT_WRITE_GRACE,
        help="seconds within which a write to a session's transcript counts as seeing it alive",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--escalate", type=float, default=600.0, help="seconds after an alert before the chief of staff is told again")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--pane-grace",
        type=float,
        default=180.0,
        help="seconds an agent tmux pane must sit at a shell with no omp before it alerts",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--move-grace",
        type=float,
        default=1800.0,
        help="seconds a mv-<worker> mover pane may sit at a shell before a move is called stuck",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--state",
        type=Path,
        default=DEFAULT_STATE,
        help="the owned state file; another path is for tests and never sends",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--dir-grace",
        type=float,
        default=DEFAULT_DIR_GRACE,
        help=(
            "seconds within which a write under the holder's working directory quiets an"
            " alert no source can clear; it never says the holder is alive"
        ),
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--budget",
        type=float,
        default=DEFAULT_BUDGET,
        help=(
            "seconds of wall clock one run may take, counted from the moment the process"
            " started; past it the run stops between two units of work, saves what it has done"
            " and exits 0, so systemd records no failure"
        ),
    )
    parser.add_argument("--http-timeout", type=float, default=8.0)
    parser.add_argument(
        "--check-hosts", nargs="+", default=list(DEFAULT_CHECK_HOSTS), metavar="HOST",
        help="SSH hosts checked before an alert when Envoy has no holder registration",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true", help="decide and log, send nothing")
    parser.add_argument("--write-state", action="store_true", help="with --dry-run, still write the state file")
    parser.add_argument("--print-alert", action="store_true", help="print the alert text it would send")
    options = parser.parse_args()
    # The once-per-incident record lives in one file. A run on any other state file has its own
    # memory of what was already said, so it may never send: that is exactly how one incident
    # produced two "first alert" messages at 07:53:20Z and 07:55:18Z on 2026-10-27.
    if options.state != DEFAULT_STATE and not options.dry_run:
        options.dry_run = True
        log(f"--state is not {DEFAULT_STATE}: running as --dry-run, sending nothing")
    try:
        return run_once(options)
    except Exception as error:  # a crashing watch must say so in the journal, not vanish
        log(f"run failed: {type(error).__name__}: {error}")
        return 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
